window.PAPERS={"2019": [{"page": 4, "text": "３   問１〜４に答えなさい。\n\n問１ 下線部の発音が他と異なるものをア～オの中から一つ選び、記号で答えなさい。\n\n\n      ア card           イ bird         ウ return       エ serve         オ worm\n\n\n\n問２ 第一アクセント(第一強勢)の位置が他と異なるものをア～オの中から一つ選び、記号\n      で答えなさい。\n\n\n      ア plastic        イ receive      ウ honest       エ coffee        オ special\n\n\n\n問３ 次の(１)、(２)が日本語の意味になるように [   ] 内の語（句）を並べかえなさい。\n   解答欄には、[    ] 内で２番目、５番目になる語（句）を記号で答えなさい。ただし、\n   文頭の文字も小文字で書かれている。\n\n（１）彼は、地元のサッカーチームに私が所属しているのかどうか聞いた。\n   [ア belonged / イ he / ウ if / エ asked / オ I / カ me / キ to ] the local soccer team.\n\n（２）彼は、ソフトの使い方をできる限り簡単な英語で説明したが、彼女は理解できなかった。\n    He explained to her how to use the software in as simple [ア but she / イ out /\n    ウ English / エ it / オ as possible / カ couldn’t figure ] .\n\n\n\n問４ 次の英文の空所に入る英語を一語ずつ答えなさい。ただし、与えられた文字から始める\n   こと。\n\n（１）When you are (h          ), you are feeling that you want to eat something.\n\n（２）A (b      ) is something that is built over a road or river so that people or cars can\n   cross from one side to the other.\n\n\n                                             3"}, {"page": 5, "text": "４   次の英文を読んで、空所（１）\n                 （２）に入る英語を答えなさい。ただし、\n                                   （１）は５語以内、\n\n    （２）は１０語以内とすること。\n\n        One day, a teacher gave his students a surprise test in his class. Once he handed\nthe test papers out, he asked the students to turn them over. Everyone was surprised to\nfind that the paper did not have any questions. There was just a black dot in the center of\nthe paper. The teacher said to them, “I want you to write about what you see there.” The\nstudents were confused, but they started on the task.\n         At the end of the test, the teacher collected all the papers. All of the students wrote\n\nabout the black dot. They tried to explain its position, its size and its color.\n         The teacher said,“I’m not going to grade you on this. I just wanted to give you\n\nsomething to think about. No one wrote about the white part of the paper. Everyone\nfocused on the black dot. The same thing happens in our lives. We sometimes only see the\nblack dot in our lives such as (             1            ), poor grades on our exams, or our\ndifficult relationship with friends. The black dots are very small when we think about\neverything we have in our lives, but (               2          ). Take your eyes away from the\nblack dots in your lives. Be positive and live a happy life!”\n\n\n                                                 4"}, {"page": 6, "text": "５     次の英文を読んで、後の問１～７に答えなさい。\n\n       It was a cold night. Mr. and Mrs. White and their son Herbert were expecting a\nvisitor.\n       “There he is,” said Herbert, as their friend Morris walked in through the gate. Mr.\nWhite answered the door and let him in. They all sat by the fire, and Morris told stories of\nhis travels.\n       “I would love to go to India myself,” said Mr. White. “What was that story about the\nmonkey’s paw* you were telling me the other day, Morris?”\n       “It was nothing,” Morris said quickly.\n       “Monkey’s paw?” said Mrs. White.\n       “It’s just a little paw.” Morris took a dried monkey paw from his pocket.\n       “What is special about it?” asked Mr. White.\n       “A very holy man put a magic spell* on it so that three different men could have\nthree wishes each from it,” said Morris. The three listeners laughed a little, and then\nHerbert asked, “Well, have you made your wishes?”\n       “I have,” Morris said, and his face turned white.\n       “Did your wishes come true?” asked Mrs. White.\n       “They did,” said Morris.\n       “Has anybody else wished?” asked Mr. White.\n       “The first man had his three wishes,” Morris replied. “I don’t know what he wished\nfor, but he died after his third wish. That’s how I got the paw.” His voice was so serious that\nthe others became very quiet.\n       “If you’ve had your three wishes, it’s ①no good to you now, Morris,” said Mr. White.\n“If you don’t need it, give it to me.”\n       “If you really want it, you can keep it,” said Morris.\n       “How do you use it?” Mr. White asked.\n       “Hold it up and wish aloud,” answered Morris. “But be careful what you wish for.”\n\n      After Morris left, Mr. White took the paw from his pocket and said, “②[          ]. It\nseems to me I’ve got all I want.”\n      “We still have to pay the money for this house, don’t we? Then, wish for two hundred\npounds,” said Mrs. White.\n      “Who would believe such a stupid story?” said Herbert.\n      “But let me just try,” said Mr. White.\n      Mr. White held up the paw and said clearly, “I wish for two hundred pounds.” Then,\nhe screamed and dropped the paw.\n      “It moved! The paw moved in my hands like a snake!”\n      “Well, I don’t see the money,” laughed his son, “and I don’t think I ever will.”\n\n      The next day, after Herbert left for work, Mrs. White checked their mail box. She\nwas ③[       ] to find nothing special in the mail. A few hours later, she became hopeful\nwhen she saw a well-dressed man walking in through the gate. ‘Maybe he brought us some\ngood news,’ she thought. Mr. and Mrs. White welcomed him in and waited for the man to\nexplain the reason he was there.\n      “I’m from your son’s company,” he said.\n                                              5"}, {"page": 7, "text": "        “Has anything happened to Herbert? What is it?” she asked.\n        “I’m sorry,” said the visitor.\n        “Is he hurt?” asked Mr. White.\n        The visitor nodded. “Badly hurt,” he said quietly, “but he is not in any pain now.”\n        “Oh, thank God!” said Mrs. White. “Thank God for that!”\n        ④“Thank…” Suddenly, she became quiet. She realized what the man meant. She\nturned to her husband. There was a long silence.\n        “It was an accident. He was caught in a machine,” said the visitor.\n        “I can’t believe this. He was the only child left to us,” Mrs. White started crying.\n        “The company is very sorry for what happened. They wish to give you money for your\nloss,” the visitor said.\n        Mr. White’s dry lips shaped the words, “How much?”\n        “Two hundred pounds,” was the answer. Mrs. White screamed as she fell to the floor.\n\n       Ten days after their son’s funeral, Mr. White woke up in the night to a sudden cry\nfrom his wife.\n       “The monkey’s paw!” she shouted. “Where is it?”\n       “It’s in my desk,” Mr. White replied. “Why?”\n       “I only just thought of it,” she said excitedly. “Why didn’t I think of it before?”\n       “Think of what?” he asked.\n       “We still have the other two wishes,” she replied quickly. “Go and get it right away,\nand wish our boy to be alive again.”\n       “You are crazy!” Mr. White cried. “He has been dead for ten days, and besides, after\nthe accident, he looked terrible.”\n       “I don’t care! Bring him back,” cried Mrs. White. “Do you think I’m scared of my own\nchild?” Mrs. White pulled her husband to the desk and found the paw.\n       “Wish!” Mrs. White cried in a strong voice.\n       ⑤“It’s a bad idea,” her husband said.\n       “Wish!” repeated his wife.\n       He finally raised his hands and said, “I wish my son alive again.” The paw fell to the\nfloor. Mr. White sat down, feeling cold with fear. His wife kept looking out the window. They\nwaited for a while, but nothing happened. Mr. White felt relieved and went back to bed. A\nfew moments later, his wife joined him. Then, they heard a knock at the door.\n       “It’s Herbert!” she screamed. She tried to run to the door, but her husband caught\nher.\n       “What are you going to do?” he said.\n       “It’s my boy. It’s Herbert!” she cried. “I must open the door.”\n       “Don’t let him in,” cried Mr. White.\n       “Let me go. I’m coming, Herbert.” Mrs. White ran out of the room. Mr. White ran and\nreached for the monkey’s paw. He held it up and said his third and last ⑥[             ].\n\n     The knocking stopped suddenly. When Mrs. White opened the door, there was\nnobody there. Mr. White heard his wife’s long loud cry of disappointment.\n\n       *paw 「\n            （動物の）手」                    *a magic spell 「呪文」\n\n                                             6"}, {"page": 8, "text": "問１ 下線部①no goodの意味として最も適切なものを次の中から一つ選び、記号で答えな\n   さい。\n   ア 信じられない     イ 止められない  ウ 手に入らない   エ 役に立たない\n\n問２ 下線部②[        ]内に入る最も適切なものを次の中から一つ選び、記号で答えなさい。\n   ア I don’t know what to wish for\n   イ I don’t know if what Morris told us is true\n   ウ I want to wish for two hundred pounds\n   エ I want to become rich and famous\n\n問３ 下線部③[       ]内に入る最も適切なものを次の中から一つ選び、記号で答えなさい。\n   ア relieved\n   イ scared\n   ウ pleased\n   エ disappointed\n\n問４ 下線部④が表現している内容として、最も適切なものを次の中から一つ選び、記号で答\n   えなさい。\n   ア 息子に全く痛みがないことに驚いている様子\n   イ 息子が無事であったことを神様に感謝している様子\n   ウ 息子が亡くなってしまったことに気づいた様子\n   エ 息子の怪我が軽傷ですんだことに安心した様子\n\n問５ 下線部⑤の理由として最も適切なものを次の中から一つ選び、記号で答えなさい。\n   ア He was afraid that his scary-looking son might come back.\n   イ He did not believe that the monkey’s paw had any special power.\n   ウ He was sure that his wife was scared of her own child.\n   エ He wanted to make his own wishes come true with the monkey’s paw.\n\n問６ 下線部⑥[           ]内に入る最も適切な語を本文中から一語で抜き出し、答えなさい。\n\n問７ 本文の内容と一致するものを次の中から二つ選び、記号で答えなさい。\n   ア Mr. White went to India and received a monkey paw from a very holy man.\n   イ Herbert did not believe that his father’s wish would become a reality.\n   ウ Mr. and Mrs. White got the money they wished for but lost something very\n     important at the same time.\n   エ Mr. White woke his wife up very excitedly when he thought of a way to have\n     their son back.\n   オ When Mr. White heard a knock at the door, he did not believe it was Herbert.\n   カ Both Mr. and Mrs. White were very disappointed that their second wish did not\n     come true.\n\n\n                                        7"}, {"page": 9, "text": "余白\n\n\n8"}, {"page": 10, "text": "６     次の英文を読んで、後の問１～８に答えなさい。\n\n      Today, the world faces an antibiotics* problem. Antibiotics are a powerful medicine.\n\nSome bacteria are bad for humans and make them sick. Antibiotics kill bad bacteria, so\n\n①そのおかげで病気の人々はまた元気になることができる。 But if the same antibiotics keep\n\nbeing used, they do not work as well. Bacteria are always changing. They change and\n\nbecome stronger. Now, there are some bacteria which ②are resistant to drugs.\n\n         (ア) Now, some scientists say that we may be able to find a solution ③right below\n\nour feet. Sean Brady is a scientist in New York studying bacteria. In the past, scientists\n\ntried to create antibiotics inside scientific laboratories. They are now looking to find them\n\nin other places. Brady believes he can find antibiotics in the ground outside. “Every place\n\nyou step, there are 10,000 bacteria,” said Brady. “Some are bad for humans, ④[              ]\n\nothers can be good if used wisely.”\n\n         (イ) In February 2018, Brady and his team announced that they discovered a new\n\nantibiotic called malacidin. It can kill several super-strong bacteria, which old antibiotics\n\ncannot fight against any more. “This discovery shows there are many helpful cures in\n\nnature,” Brady says. “We just need to find them.”\n\n         (ウ) Then, they used new technology and studied the soil* that had lots of bacteria\n\nand continued searching for something interesting in the DNA of those bacteria. “Most of\n\nwhat’s there is completely unknown, and ⑤that’s the future,” Brady said. For example,\n\nBrady and his team found a special gene that could help attack bad bacteria. His team\n\nmade a new antibiotic from the soil and tested it on sick rats. The rats got better, and the\n\nbad bacteria did not show up any longer.\n\n         (エ) Now, Brady wants to keep testing and developing this cure so that it can be\n\nused for humans in the future. Other scientists are using the same methods as Brady. They\n\nwant to ⑥[       ] new antibiotics in ocean water or insects.\n\n      *antibiotics「抗生物質」               *soil「土」\n\n                                             9"}, {"page": 11, "text": "問１ 下線部①の日本語とほぼ同じ内容になるように、次の英文の空所に入る英語を一語ずつ\n   答えなさい。\n   they (    1   )(     2    ) people (   3    ) well (   4    ).\n\n\n問２ 下線部②の意味に最も近いものを次の中から一つ選び、記号で答えなさい。\n   ア keep in touch with                        イ are weaker than\n   ウ do not destroy                            エ are not killed by\n\n\n問３ 下線部③の説明として最も適切なものを次の中から一つ選び、記号で答えなさい。\n   ア inside the shoes                          イ in an earlier time\n   ウ inside scientific laboratories            エ in the soil\n\n\n問４ 下線部④[          ]に入るものを次の中から一つ選び、記号で答えなさい。\n   ア while            イ so            ウ because                エ if\n\n\n問５ 下線部⑤の説明として最も適切なものを次の中から一つ選び、記号で答えなさい。\n   ア We do not know what we will study in the future.\n   イ We have already finished what we can do now.\n   ウ We have a good chance of discovering something new.\n   エ We are no longer interested in the past research.\n\n\n問６ 下線部⑥[          ]に入る最も適切な語を本文中から一語で抜き出し、答えなさい。\n\n\n問７ 次の英文を入れるのに最も適切な箇所を本文中の(ア) ～ (エ)の中から一つ選び、記号で\n   答えなさい。\n   For this study, Brady’s team collected soil from across the country.\n\n\n問８ 本文の内容と一致するものを次の中から一つ選び、記号で答えなさい。\n   ア We can make drugs stronger if we keep using them again and again.\n   イ Some antibiotics do not work for certain types of bacteria any longer.\n   ウ Malacidin was tested on rats, and it killed them right away.\n   エ Brady thinks it will be too dangerous to use antibiotics from the soil for humans\n       in the future.\n\n                                              10"}], "2020": [{"page": 4, "text": "３   問１〜４に答えなさい。\n\n問１ 下線部の発音が他と異なるものをア～エの中から一つ選び、記号で答えなさい。\n\n\n      ア leave        イ cheap         ウ dead           エ sea\n\n\n\n問２ 第一アクセント(第一強勢)の位置が他と異なるものをア～エの中から一つ選び、記号\n      で答えなさい。\n\n\n      ア damage        イ escape        ウ against       エ instead\n\n\n\n問３ 次の(１)、(２)が日本語の意味になるように [   ] 内の語（句）を並べかえなさい。\n   解答欄には、[    ] 内で２番目、５番目になる語（句）を記号で答えなさい。ただし、\n   文頭の文字も小文字で書かれている。\n\n（１）昨日、家に帰る途中で会った友達と 1 時間以上もおしゃべりをしてしまった。\n    Yesterday I saw [ ア of / イ more than / ウ a chat with him for / エ a friend /\n    オ my way home / カ on / キ and had / ク mine ] an hour.\n\n（２）スミスさんが日本に来た理由をご存知ですか。\n   [ ア Mr. Smith / イ know / ウ to / エ do / オ what / カ brought / キ you ] Japan?\n\n\n問４ 次の英文の空所に入る英語を一語ずつ答えなさい。ただし、与えられた文字から始める\n   こと。\n\n（１）If something is (u        ), it will help you to do or get what you want.\n\n（２）The (w       ) is the condition of the atmosphere in one area at a particular time; for\n   example, it is raining, hot, or windy.\n\n\n                                             3"}, {"page": 5, "text": "４   次の英文を読んで、空所（１）\n                 （２）に入る英語を答えなさい。ただし、\n                                   （１）は５語以内、\n\n    （２）は１５語以内とすること。\n\n       Once upon a time, a hunter had a very intelligent dog. The dog saved him from wild\nanimals many times while they were hunting in the forest. The dog loved the hunter’s\nfamily, too. It often played with the hunter’s baby and took care of him. One day, the\nhunter’s wife was not at home, and the hunter had to go out to hunt. So he left his\none-year-old son alone at home with the dog.\n       While the hunter was hunting, a wolf came into the house and made its way to the\n\nbaby’s room. Suddenly, the dog noticed something unusual was happening and went to the\nroom. The wolf was going to eat the baby. After a long, hard fight, the dog (   1     ).\n       When the hunter returned home in the evening, he met the dog at the door and saw\nblood on its nose. The hunter entered the house and saw more blood in the house. He\nthought the dog had killed the baby. He got so angry that he picked up his gun and shot the\ndog dead.\n       As soon as he entered the baby’s room, he found his child was safe. A dead wolf was\nlying near the baby. The hunter realized his terrible mistake and couldn’t stop crying. He\nfelt very sorry for what he had done, but it was too late.\n       This story teaches you the lesson that (          2          ).\n\n\n                                               4"}, {"page": 6, "text": "５     次の英文を読んで、後の問１～７に答えなさい。\n\n        One summer thirty years ago, Sheila Mant was the only thing I loved more than\nbass*. I was fourteen. Sheila’s family rented the holiday house next to ours by the river.\nThey often held parties, and I would often go out to look at Sheila in their lovely garden.\nSheila was the middle daughter—at seventeen. She would spend her days relaxing by the\nriver, and when she entered the water, many boys would come near to talk to her.\n        I was on the swim team at school, and in order to ①[     ] her, I would swim many\nlaps in front of her house, hoping she would look at me. Fishing was another thing I was\ngood at. When I was out fishing on the river, I would look toward her, but she was never\nwatching me. So I climbed the diving board* and did my best dive into the river and kept\non diving until she left.\n\n        One day in late August, I finally gathered enough courage to ask her out on a date.\nSheila and her family were playing softball outside. She was standing halfway between the\nfirst and second base. She didn’t seem surprised to see me—in fact, she didn’t seem to see\nme at all.\n        “If you’re playing second base, you should move closer.” I said. Sheila’s beautiful\nface turned to me.\n        “I’m playing outfield*,” she said. “I don’t like the responsibility of having a base.”\n        “Yeah, I can understand that,” I said, although I couldn’t. “There’s a band playing in\nDixford tomorrow night. Want to go?”\n        “You have a car?” she said.\n        “We’ll go by canoe.”\n        I was very happy when she agreed. I spent all of the following day cleaning my\ncanoe. I carried it into the water, putting cushions so Sheila could sit on them. I also placed\nmy father’s radio in the middle so we could have music. Without thinking about it, I\nprepared my fishing rod* and fixed it on the canoe. I always took my fishing rod wherever I\nwent that summer.\n        It was already dark by the time I arrived at Sheila’s house near the river by canoe.\nTen minutes later, Sheila appeared. She looked very beautiful in her white dress. She\nlooked at my canoe and said, “Look, ②[            ].”\n        “It’s faster this way,” I lied. “Hey, don’t worry. It’s safe.”\n        She stepped into the canoe unhappily. I was glad she wasn’t facing me. When her\neyes were on me, I was too nervous to speak.\n        I started paddling*. There was an extra paddle, but Sheila made no move to pick it\nup. She took her shoes off and put her feet over the side. Ten minutes went by.\n        “What’s that sound?” Sheila said, pointing toward the water.\n        “That’s bass. They come into the shallow water at night to catch frogs,” I said\nproudly.\n        “I think fishing is stupid,” she said. “I mean, it’s boring. Definitely stupid.”\n        I didn’t understand why Sheila hated fishing so much. Was her father a fisherman?\nDid she try it once and get bored? It didn’t matter. The important thing now was that I had\nto ③conceal my love for fishing so that I wouldn’t look stupid.\n        “What kind of band is playing tonight?” she said.\n                                              5"}, {"page": 7, "text": "        “It’s sort of like country music. You’ll like it.”\n        “Eric Caswell is going to be there.”\n        “Really?” I said though I had no idea who that was.\n        Suddenly, my rod started to bend* down. It was a bass, and it was the biggest one I\nhad ever seen.\n        “What was the noise?” she said, turning around.\n        ④“Uh…wild ducks?” I said.\n        Slowly, not making any motion that might draw Sheila’s attention, I held the\nfishing rod.\n        Sheila started talking. “I went to a party at the college over there. My sister says I\ncould get in the college if I wanted. But I’m thinking about going somewhere I can ski.”\n        I was busy trying to catch the big bass and listen to what Sheila was saying at the\nsame time.\n        “That’s great,” I answered. “Skiing. Yeah, I can see that.”\n        “My friends said I can be a model, but I thought I should get an education first. I\nmean, it might be a while before I start my modeling career.”\n        She paused.\n        “Are we going backward?”\n        We were. The big bass was pulling the canoe. I could feel the weight of the great\nbass. If I stood up and started pulling the rod, I would have the bass. Then, I looked at\nSheila. Her beautiful long hair was waving in the wind. ⑤I took a knife from my pocket\nand cut the fishing line. With a sick feeling in my stomach, I saw the rod unbend.\n        “I’m tired,” Sheila said. “Are we there yet?”\n        With a great effort of self-control, I was able to bring the canoe to the shore and\nhelp Sheila out. I don’t remember much about the rest of the night. We walked to the music\nfestival, and there was the smell of popcorn and the sound of guitars. I danced once or twice\nwith Sheila, but once the music was finished, she explained that she would go home in Eric\nCaswell’s car.\n        “Okay,” I said quietly.\n        For the first time that night, she looked at me and said, “You’re a funny boy, you\nknow that?”\n\n        Before the summer was over, Sheila’s spell* over me was gone, but the memory of\nlosing the ⑥[      ] never disappeared. I decided not to make the same mistake ever again.\n\n*bass「バス(スズキ科の魚)」                      *diving board 「飛び込み台」\n*play outfield「外野を守る」                  *fishing rod「釣ざお」\n*paddling「パドルでこぐこと」                    *bend「曲がる」            *spell「魔法」\n\n\n                                              6"}, {"page": 8, "text": "問１ 下線部①[            ]内に入る最も適切なものを次の中から一つ選び、記号で答えなさい。\n   ア impress         イ accept ウ upset エ encourage\n\n問２ 下線部②[         ]内に入る最も適切なものを次の中から一つ選び、記号で答えなさい。\n   ア going by canoe is faster\n   イ you have a beautiful canoe\n   ウ I hate driving a car\n   エ we can use my father’s car\n\n問３ 下線部③concealの意味として最も適切なものを次の中から一つ選び、記号で答えな\n   さい。\n   ア 自慢する     イ 隠す   ウ 理解する エ 深める\n\n問４ 下線部④が表現している内容として、最も適切なものを次の中から一つ選び、記号で答\n   えなさい。\n   ア 突然鳥が飛んできたことに驚いている様子\n   イ 何が大きな音を立てたか分からずに戸惑っている様子\n   ウ 女の子に自分の釣りの腕前を見せようと頑張っている様子\n   エ 大きな魚がかかったことをごまかそうとしている様子\n\n問５ 下線部⑤の理由として最も適切なものを次の中から一つ選び、記号で答えなさい。\n   ア He decided to give up the bass for the girl he liked.\n   イ He was feeling very sick from the canoe ride.\n   ウ He was sure that he would not be able to catch the bass.\n   エ He wanted to surprise the girl with his fishing skills.\n\n問６ 下線部⑥[            ]内に入る最も適切な語を本文中から一語で抜き出し、答えなさい。\n\n問７ 本文の内容と一致するものを次の中から二つ選び、記号で答えなさい。\n   ア Sheila often went to the river for a swim because she wanted to be a good\n     swimmer.\n   イ The writer gathered courage to invite Sheila to play softball with his family.\n   ウ The writer spent a lot of time preparing for the date he was going on with Sheila.\n   エ Sheila realized that the writer was trying to catch the bass when he stopped\n     talking to her.\n   オ The writer and Sheila listened to the radio on the way home from the music\n     festival.\n   カ Although the writer tried hard to win Sheila’s love, he was not successful.\n\n\n                                          7"}, {"page": 9, "text": "余白\n\n\n8"}, {"page": 10, "text": "６      次の英文を読んで、後の問１～８に答えなさい。\n     Brett Treichel’s mother gave him a piece of peanut butter toast when he was 9 months\nold. Brett touched the peanut butter, and then his eye. Suddenly, his eye started to swell*.\nBrett was taken to the hospital. Doctors said he had a peanut allergy.\n     Now Brett is 13 years old and has learned to live with his allergy. In the school\ncafeteria, he sits far from classmates who are eating peanut butter. His school keeps a\nmedical device called an EpiPen in the front office. His teachers are trained to use it.\nBrett’s doctors say that, like most kids who are allergic to peanuts, ①he probably won’t\ngrow out of his allergy. “I have to be careful for life,” Brett says.\n     A recent study in the journal called Pediatrics found that one in 50 children in the\nUnited States is allergic to peanuts. That’s 1.6 million kids. For many, a very small amount\nof peanut can ②trigger a reaction called anaphylaxis. It leads to a swelling of the tongue\nand throat. An EpiPen can stop the reaction. But if not treated, anaphylaxis can kill them.\n     (ア) There is no known cure for a peanut allergy. However, a company called Aimmune\nTherapeutics started to develop a treatment to help children with peanut allergies. 554\nchildren participated in their study. Aimmune spent a year conducting the research in\norder to make sure the treatment was safe and effective.\n     (イ) A child is given just one milligram of peanut powder. For most patients, this small\namount of peanut does not trigger an allergic reaction. Then, more peanut powder is added\ndaily. Over time, the patients will be able to eat more and more amounts of peanut without\nhaving allergic reactions.\n     (ウ) Aimmune Therapeutics announced the results in November 2018. ③More than\n96% of the children who finished the treatment could handle at least 300 milligrams of\npeanut powder. That’s equal to about one peanut. The findings could now mean that help is\non the way for Brett and other allergy patients.\n     (エ) Sayantani Sindher from Stanford University is one of the researchers who did\nthis study. Sindher said that even after the treatment, kids must stay away from peanuts.\n④[       ], thanks to the treatment, they can avoid a reaction that may kill them. “It’s\nbite-proof protection,” Sindher said. “So if you accidentally take a bite of a cookie with\n⑤[       ] in it, you don’t have to run to the hospital. It gives peace of mind to kids and\nparents, and improves their quality of life.”\n     Indeed, although peanut treatment sounds attractive to many patients, it has to be\ndone with a doctor’s advice. Patients are closely watched all the time for signs of an allergic\nreaction. ⑥経験豊かな医者がいないところで、アレルギーを持っている食べ物を摂取するこ\nとは極めて危険である。 If families think this treatment could be right for them, they\nshould see a doctor.\n\n\n     *swell「腫れる」\n\n                                                9"}, {"page": 11, "text": "問１ 下線部①の説明として最も適切なものを次の中から一つ選び、記号で答えなさい。\n   ア Brett will have to live with his allergy throughout his life.\n   イ Brett will learn to take care of his allergy when he gets older.\n   ウ Brett will not be able to become an adult because of his allergy.\n   エ Brett will not suffer from his allergy when he grows up.\n\n問２ 下線部②の意味に最も近いものを次の中から一つ選び、記号で答えなさい。\n   ア block イ survive ウ cause    エ harm\n\n問３ 下線部③の説明として最も適切なものを次の中から一つ選び、記号で答えなさい。\n   ア Many of the kids have no trouble at all with their peanut allergies now.\n   イ Almost all the kids can now eat 300 peanuts a day without any serious problems.\n   ウ Few of the kids can now have 300 milligrams of peanut powder in a day.\n   エ Most of the kids will not show allergic reactions even if they have one peanut now.\n\n問４ 下線部④[        ]に入るものを次の中から一つ選び、記号で答えなさい。\n   ア As a result イ For example ウ In other words エ However\n\n問５ 下線部⑤[            ]に入る最も適切な語を本文中から一語で抜き出し、答えなさい。\n\n問６ 下線部⑥の日本語とほぼ同じ内容になるように、次の英文の空所に入る英語を一語ずつ\n   答えなさい。\n   ( 1 ) an experienced doctor, ( 2 ) is extremely ( 3 ) to eat a food you are\n   ( 4 ) to.\n\n問７ 次の英文を入れるのに最も適切な箇所を本文中の(ア) ～ (エ)の中から一つ選び、記号で\n   答えなさい。\n    Here’s how the treatment works.\n\n問８ 本文の内容と一致するものを次の中から一つ選び、記号で答えなさい。\n   ア Brett always tries to stay closer to his classmates in the school cafeteria.\n   イ A recent study found that 2% of kids in the US have a peanut allergy.\n   ウ EpiPens kill many kids with anaphylaxis in the US every year.\n   エ Sindher said that it is completely safe to eat peanuts after the treatment.\n\n\n                                           10"}], "2021": [{"page": 6, "text": "３   問１〜４に答えなさい。\n\n問１ 下線部の発音が他と異なるものを次の中から一つ選び、記号で答えなさい。\n\n\n       ア language         イ happen               ウ act               エ radio\n\n\n問２ 第一アクセント(第一強勢)の位置が他と異なるものを次の中から一つ選び、記号で答\n      えなさい。\n\n\n       ア difference       イ volunteer            ウ government        エ excellent\n\n\n\n問３ 次の(１)、(２)が日本語の意味になるように [   ] 内の語（句）を並べかえなさい。\n   解答欄には、[    ] 内で２番目、５番目になる語（句）を記号で答えなさい。\n\n（１）夜更かしをする人は風邪をひきやすいと言われている。\n    It is said that [ア who /イ late /ウ a cold /エ people /オ may catch /カ up /キ stay] easily.\n\n\n\n（２）物事が自分の思い通りにならないからと言って、動揺しないようにするにはどうすれば\n   良いだろうか。\n    How [ア things /イ upset /ウ when /エ do /オ I /カ getting /キ stop] don’t go my way?\n\n\n\n問４ 次の英文の空所に入る英語を一語ずつ答えなさい。ただし、与えられた文字から始める\n   こと。\n\n\n（１）A ( v        ) is a holiday or time spent not working.\n\n\n\n（２）To ( p        ) is to make something or somebody ready to be used or to do something.\n\n\n                                             3"}, {"page": 7, "text": "４   次の英文を読んで、空所（１）（２）に入る英語を答えなさい。ただし、いずれの空所も\n\n    １０語以内とすること。\n\n\n        One day when an old man was walking along the beach, he saw that the beach was\n\ncovered with tens of thousands of starfish. Far down the beach, he found a young girl who\n\nwas picking the starfish up, one by one, and tossing them back into the ocean.\n\n        The man became curious about her and walked over to the girl to speak with her.\n\n        “Little girl,” the old man said, “what are you doing?”\n\n        “I’m saving these starfish,” said the girl. “If I don’t throw them back in the water,\n\nthey will die. They need water to live.”\n\n        The old man laughed and thought to himself ‘stupid girl.’ Then he said, “But you\n\nare only one person. There are tens of thousands of starfish on this beach. What a waste of\ntime! Your effort will make no difference.”\n\n        The girl picked up another starfish, looked at it, looked up at the man, tossed it into\n\nthe ocean, and then said, “It will to that one.”\n\n        The girl in the story teaches you a lesson that (    1     ) even if (   2     ).\n\n\n                                               4"}, {"page": 8, "text": "５     次の英文を読んで、後の問１～７に答えなさい。\n\n         The house stood on the top of a low hill in a small village in the Mexican\ncountryside. There lived a farmer named Lencho. He worked every day on his farm\ngrowing corn and beans. His plants grew nicely this year, and the only thing the earth\nneeded before the harvest* season was some rain. One morning, Lencho looked at the sky\nfrom his window and prayed to God.\n         “①[        ],” Lencho said to his wife, who was preparing lunch.\n         “How do you know?” she asked.\n         “God will surely help us because I prayed,” replied Lencho.\n         The older children were working on the farm, while the smaller ones were playing\nnear the house, until the wife called to them all.\n         “Come for lunch.”\n         During the meal, just as Lencho had told his wife, dark clouds covered the sky, and\nbig drops of rain began to fall. The air was fresh and sweet. Lencho went outside to feel the\nrain on his body. When he returned to his house, he said proudly, “Those aren’t raindrops\nfalling from the sky. They are silver coins. The big drops are ten-cent coins and the little\nones are fives....”\n         Lencho was very happy now as he knew that he was able to make a lot of money by\nselling his corn and beans.\n         Then, suddenly, a strong wind began to blow, and very large hailstones* began to\nfall. These truly looked like silver coins.\n         “It’s really getting bad now,” shouted Lencho, “I hope it stops quickly.”\n         But it did not. For an hour, the hailstones fell on the house and the farm. The farm\nwas white. It looked like it was covered with salt. Not a single leaf remained on the trees.\nThe corn was totally destroyed. The flowers were gone from the bean plants. Lencho’s heart\nwas filled with sadness. When the storm passed, he stood in the middle of the farm and\nsaid to his children, “Look, the hailstorm has left nothing. This year, we will have no corn\nor beans. We worked hard for nothing! We are all going to ②starve.” That night, Lencho\ncouldn’t go to sleep. With his heavy heart, he was thinking to himself, ‘We are in great\ntrouble. There is nothing we can do, and no one can help us.’\n         Then, Lencho’s face suddenly brightened as he found a hope, a single hope, which\nwould save him and his family. His only hope was help from God.\n         Even though Lencho was a simple farmer, he still knew how to write. The next\nmorning, he began to write a letter. It was a letter to God.\n         ‘God,’ he wrote, ‘I lost all of my corn and bean plants because of the terrible\nhailstorm. If you don’t help me, my family and I will go hungry this year. I need a hundred\npesos* in order to buy new seeds for my farm and to be able to eat until my corn and beans\ngrow again.’\n         He wrote ‘To God’ on the envelope, put the letter inside and went to town. At the\npost office, he placed a stamp on the envelope and dropped it into the mailbox. One of the\npostmen in the post office found Lencho’s letter and started laughing. He brought the letter\nto his boss and said, “I have never delivered a letter to God in my life!”\n         The boss, who was a big, kind-hearted man, also started laughing, but quickly he\nturned serious and said, “What faith*! I have never met a man who has more faith in God\n                                             5"}, {"page": 9, "text": "than the man who wrote this letter. We should not ③[             ] this man by telling him that\nhis letter cannot be delivered. Oh, I have just come up with a great idea. Why don’t we\nwrite a letter from God and deliver it to him?”\n         However, when the boss opened the envelope and read the letter, he realized that\n④he needed something more than ink and paper.\n         “Shall we just tell the man that the letter cannot be delivered?” asked the postman.\n         “Wait, we can still collect some money and help the man,” answered the boss.\n         The boss asked all the workers in the post office to give a little money for an act of\nkindness, and he himself donated a large amount of money from his salary. However, it was\nimpossible for him to gather a hundred pesos. He was able to give the farmer only a little\nmore than half. He put the money in the envelope together with a letter saying, ‘From\n⑤[        ].’\n         The following Sunday, Lencho came early in the morning to ask if there was a letter\nfor him. The postman handed the letter to Lencho. The boss was watching them from the\ndoorway of his office with a big smile on his face as he imagined Lencho’s reaction. Lencho\ndidn’t show any surprise when he opened the envelope, but his face turned red when he\ncounted the money.\n         “God cannot make any mistakes!” he shouted.\n         Quickly, Lencho went up to the counter to ask for ink and paper. On the public\nwriting table, he started to write a letter. When he finished, ⑥he walked very quickly to\nthe counter to buy a stamp and attached it to the envelope by hitting it many times with\nhis fist. As soon as the letter fell into the mailbox, the boss went to open it. It said:\n\nDear God,\n\nOf the money that I asked for, only seventy pesos reached me. Send me the rest, because I\nneed it very much. But don’t send it to me through the mail because the post-office workers\nare thieves.\n\n                                                                                 Lencho\n\n\n\n*harvest「収獲」                           *hailstone「雹 (ひょう)の粒」\n*peso「ペソ (メキシコの通貨)」                    *faith「信仰心」\n\n\n                                              6"}, {"page": 10, "text": "問１ 下線部①[         ]内に入る最も適切なものを次の中から一つ選び、記号で答えなさい。\n   ア Now we really need some rain\n   イ Now we’re going to get some rain\n   ウ I don’t think God will help us\n   エ I don’t think our corn and beans will grow\n\n\n問２ 下線部②starve の意味として最も適切なものを次の中から一つ選び、記号で答えなさい。\n   ア 飢えに苦しむ       イ 神に祈る     ウ 種を植える   エ 必死に働く\n\n\n問３ 下線部③[       ]内に入る最も適切なものを次の中から一つ選び、記号で答えなさい。\n   ア encourage      イ entertain ウ disappoint エ dislike\n\n\n問４ 下線部④の理由として最も適切なものを次の中から一つ選び、記号で答えなさい。\n   ア Lencho was asking God for some money in his letter.\n   イ Lencho was planning to write another letter to God.\n   ウ The boss needed to write a letter from God.\n   エ The boss didn’t have any ink and paper.\n\n\n問５ 下線部⑤[           ]内に入る最も適切な語を本文中から一語で抜き出し、答えなさい。\n\n\n問６ 下線部⑥が表現している内容として、最も適切なものを次の中から一つ選び、記号で答\n   えなさい。\n   ア 神様が本当に手紙の返事を書いてくれたので驚いている様子\n   イ 神様が間違った金額を封筒に入れたことに気づき慌てている様子\n   ウ 郵便局の従業員がお金を恵んでくれたことに感謝をしている様子\n   エ 郵便局の従業員にお金を盗まれたと思い込みいら立っている様子\n\n\n問７ 本文の内容と一致するものを次の中から二つ選び、記号で答えなさい。\n   ア Lencho and his family were able to make a lot of money because silver coins\n     started falling from the sky.\n   イ Lencho never learned how to write because he was living on a farm for all of his\n     life.\n   ウ The boss thought that the man who wrote the letter to God was crazy, so he\n     decided to play a trick on him.\n   エ When Lencho came back to the post office, the boss smiled because he expected\n     that Lencho would be happy to receive the money.\n   オ Lencho was so pleased to receive a letter from God that he wrote another letter\n     to him.\n   カ Even though Lencho didn’t receive the amount of money he wanted from God, he\n     didn’t lose his faith in God.\n                                         7"}, {"page": 11, "text": "余白\n\n\n8"}, {"page": 12, "text": "６        次の英文を読んで、後の問１～８に答えなさい。\n        Corals* are dying worldwide because of rising sea temperatures. As corals die, the sea\ncreatures who live around them move on. Now scientists have found an unusual way to get\nfish back – setting up ①[        ] in the ocean.\n        （ア）As the temperatures of the world’s oceans have increased in recent years, corals\naround the world have begun to die off. This is often called “coral bleaching” because\nsometimes the dying corals turn white.\n        （イ）These large living collections attract and support many other sea creatures. So\n②when corals die off, it’s not just about the corals. The creatures that live in and around\nthem may also lose their habitats. The richness of all these different kinds of animals in\nthe same place is very important to the health of oceans.\n        （ウ）Scientists from the UK and Australia were curious about how important sound\nwas for making fish come to coral reefs*. They wanted to see if they could ③lure fish to an\narea with dead corals by playing back recorded sounds from a healthy coral reef. Scientists\ncall this method “acoustic enrichment” – making an area richer in sound. The scientists\nhave recently published their results in the magazine Nature.\n        （エ）Many people think of the underwater world as silent. ④[                ], that’s not\nactually true. There are many different kinds of animals, and they make an interesting\nmixture of sounds as they go about their daily lives.\n        The scientists decided to test their idea at the Great Barrier Reef. That’s the world’s\nlargest coral reef and home to many sea animals. But it’s also seriously threatened* by the\nwarming waters caused by climate change.\n        The scientists set up piles of dead corals in three different places around the Great\nBarrier Reef. At the first location, they did nothing. At the second one, they put underwater\nloudspeakers but didn’t play anything through the loudspeakers. Around the final site,\nthey placed underwater loudspeakers and played recordings of sounds from a healthy reef\nevery night. The scientists did the experiment for six weeks. At the end of those six weeks,\n⑤the scientists found that the noisy site [         1   ] [   2   ] [   3   ] many fish as the\n[   4      ] sites. This site also had 50% more different kinds of fish. The fish that came to\nthe noisy site came faster and they stayed longer.\n        The scientists believe their experiment shows that acoustic enrichment is one\npossible way to get sea life to come back to areas where coral reefs have been damaged. The\nscientists point out that ⑥their method doesn’t make the coral reefs healthy again. But\nattracting more fish may be helpful to cure damaged reefs.\n\n\n        *corals サンゴ          *coral reefs サンゴ礁            *threatened 脅かされる\n\n                                                9"}, {"page": 13, "text": "問１ 下線部①[         ]に入る最も適切な語を本文中から一語で抜き出し、答えなさい。\n\n問２ 下線部②の説明として最も適切なものを次の中から一つ選び、記号で答えなさい。\n   ア Many animals need to support the corals when the corals are not healthy.\n   イ The corals cannot live without having sea creatures around them.\n   ウ Some animals living near the corals may no longer have a place to live.\n   エ Researchers are studying sea animals as well as the corals they live in.\n\n問３ 下線部③lure の意味に最も近いものを次の中から一つ選び、記号で答えなさい。\n   ア create        イ attract              ウ show                 エ damage\n\n\n\n問４ 下線部④[         ]に入るものを次の中から一つ選び、記号で答えなさい。\n   ア However       イ For example          ウ In other words       エ As a result\n\n問５ 下線部⑤について、次の日本語とほぼ同じ内容になるように、下線部内の空所に入る英語を\n   一語ずつ答えなさい。\n   「音のする場所には、静かなところに比べて、倍の数の魚がいたことを科学者たちは発見した」\n\n\n\n問６ 下線部⑥の内容の説明として最も適切なものを次の中から一つ選び、記号で答えなさい。\n   ア making sites rich in sound to bring back sea creatures\n   イ setting up piles of dead corals in many different places\n   ウ damaging the coral reefs by raising sea temperatures\n   エ helping the corals in the ocean to be healthy again\n\n問７ 次の英文を入れるのに最も適切な箇所を本文中の(ア) ～ (エ)の中から一つ選び、記号で答え\n   なさい。\n   The coral shapes are built over a long time by millions and millions of tiny coral\n   animals.\n\n問８ 本文の内容と一致するものを次の中から一つ選び、記号で答えなさい。\n   ア Coral bleaching is one of the ways to save dying corals by turning them white.\n   イ In order to keep the ocean healthy, it is important to have different kinds of\n      animals in the same place.\n   ウ The ocean is so silent that fish will go away when they hear a slight sound near\n      them.\n   エ The scientists in the study played recorded sounds all day long in the ocean.\n\n\n                                         10"}], "2022": [{"page": 6, "text": "３   問１〜４に答えなさい。\n\n問１ 下線部の発音が他と異なるものを次の中から一つ選び、記号で答えなさい。\n\n\n       ア thousand        イ either           ウ through                エ math\n\n\n問２ 第一アクセント(第一強勢)の位置が他と異なるものを次の中から一つ選び、記号で答\n      えなさい。\n\n\n       ア separate         イ festival        ウ expensive              エ president\n\n\n\n問３ 次の(１)、(２)が日本語の意味になるように [   ] 内の語（句）を並べかえなさ\n   い。解答欄には、[    ] 内で２番目、５番目になる語（句）を記号で答えなさい。\n\n（１）私は彼女に何と言ってよいかわからない。\n    I [ア say / イ idea / ウ I / エ have / オ what / カ should / キ no / ク to] her.\n\n（２）私の弟は 1 年付き合っている女性と結婚する予定です。\n    My brother will [ア he / イ to / ウ the woman / エ been / オ get / カ has /\n    キ married] dating for a year.\n\n\n問４ 次の英文の空所に入る英語を一語ずつ答えなさい。ただし、与えられた文字から始め\n   ること。\n\n（１）A (g     ) is a room or hall with equipment for doing physical exercise, for example\n   in a school.\n\n（２）To (i       ) is to make something better, or to become better.\n\n\n                                            3"}, {"page": 7, "text": "４   次の英文を読んで、英文の要約(summary)を４０語以内の英語で書きなさい。\n\n\n    There was a man who was illiterate in a village. He often saw people wearing glasses for\nreading books or papers. He thought, ‘If I have glasses, I can also read like these people. I\nmust go to town and buy a pair for myself.’\n    So one day he went to town. He entered a shop. He asked the shopkeeper for a pair of\nglasses for reading. The shopkeeper showed him various pairs of glasses and a book. The\nvillager tried all the pairs of glasses on one by one. But he could not read anything with them.\n    He told the shopkeeper that none of the glasses helped him to read. The shopkeeper gave\nhim a doubtful look. Then he looked at the book. It was upside down! The shopkeeper said,\n“Perhaps you don’t know how to read.”\n    The villager said, “I want to buy a pair of glasses so that I can read like others. But I\ncan’t read with any of your glasses.”\n    The shopkeeper laughed as he realized the actual problem that his customer had.\n“Glasses are useless if you are illiterate. They only help you to see better. Your real problem\nis that you do not know how to read.”\n\n\n                                               4"}, {"page": 8, "text": "５      次の英文を読んで、後の問１～７に答えなさい。\n\n       Mrs Packletide was an English woman who lived in India. She was not a brave person\nby nature, but she had a strong desire to shoot a tiger. This was not because of her interest\nin hunting but because of her ①detestation for her neighbour, Loona Bimberton. Loona\nBimberton had recently travelled to Africa in a private airplane and talked only about her\ngreat adventure. Mrs Packletide was sick of hearing her stories and wanted to have\nsomething better to show off. Therefore, she had decided to kill a tiger herself and bring home\nits skin.\n       She was confident that her successful hunting story would appear in a British\nnewspaper. She wanted to invite Loona Bimberton to her lunch party after the hunting trip.\n‘She will be so jealous because everyone will be talking about my great achievement*,’ she\nthought to herself. In addition, she had a plan to have a tiger-claw brooch* made for Loona\nBimberton’s next birthday.\n       Mrs Packletide wanted to find a way to shoot a tiger without taking much risk, so she\nwent to a nearby village where she heard that people often saw an old, dying tiger. She\noffered a thousand rupees* to the villagers for helping her to shoot the tiger.\n       The villagers were all so excited about the offer as a thousand rupees was a lot of\nmoney. They tried their best to keep the tiger in a good location so that Mrs Packletide could\nfind the tiger easily. Many village children were also asked to watch the tiger so that it would\nnot escape from the town. Even some goats* were placed here and there to feed the tiger.\nMothers were also asked to avoid singing songs to their babies so as not to disturb* the tiger’s\nsleep. The only concern that villagers had was that the tiger might die because of old age\nbefore Mrs Packletide had a chance to shoot it.\n       Mrs Packletide also paid a lot of money to a young English woman, Miss Mebbin, who\nagreed to be her hunting assistant. On the night of the hunt, the moon was bright, and there\nwere no clouds in the sky. It was the perfect night for shooting the tiger. The villagers set up\na hunting area with a tree where Mrs Packletide and Miss Mebbin could hide. A goat was\ntied at the correct distance from the tree to attract the old tiger.\n       ②“[      ]” asked Miss Mebbin.\n       “Of course not!” said Mrs Packletide. “It’s a very old tiger. It couldn’t jump up here\neven if it wanted to.”\n       “If it’s an old tiger, I think you should get it for a lower price. A thousand rupees is a\nlot of money,” said Miss Mebbin. Before Mrs Packletide could disagree, the tiger showed up.\nIt gradually started walking towards the goat.\n       “Now, shoot!” shouted Miss Mebbin with excitement. Mrs Packletide fired a shot with\nher rifle and the tiger fell to the ground. The villagers celebrated by beating drums and\nsinging. Mrs Packletide was very proud of her own achievement.\n\n\n                                                5"}, {"page": 9, "text": "         However, when Miss Mebbin looked closely at the dead tiger, she found no blood on\nits body. Then, she looked at the goat, which was also lying motionless on the ground.\n         “Mrs Packletide, you shot the wrong animal, and it seems that the poor old tiger died\nfrom a heart attack. It was probably caused by the loud bang of your rifle,” said Miss Mebbin.\nMrs Packletide was very ③[            ] the discovery. However, she tried to stay positive by\nsaying, “Well, at least I now have tiger skin to take home.” Also, the villagers all agreed to\nkeep it secret as they were excited to get the money. Therefore, Mrs Packletide was able to\nanswer questions with a light heart when journalists came to interview her.\n         A few days later, Mrs Packletide’s story appeared in a British newspaper, which Loona\nBimberton refused to look at for weeks. She didn’t even write her letter of thanks for the gift\nof a tiger-claw brooch. Then, of course, she didn’t accept the invitation to Mrs Packletide’s\nlunch party.\n         People who came to Mrs Packletide’s lunch party admired the beautiful tiger-skin rug.\n“How brave you are to go hunting such a big tiger!” one guest praised Mrs Packletide.\n         “I can’t imagine risking my life to shoot a tiger. The biggest animal I have ever hunted\nis a rabbit,” said another.\n\n\n\n         A few days after the party, Miss Mebbin said, “Everyone would be surprised to know\nthe truth.”\n         “What do you mean?” asked Mrs Packletide quickly.\n         “How you shot the goat and shocked the ④[         ] to death,” said Miss Mebbin, with a\nlaugh.\n         “No one would believe it,” said Mrs Packletide, with her face turning red.\n         “Loona Bimberton would believe it,” said Miss Mebbin. Mrs Packletide’s face was\nchanging color from red to white.\n         ⑤“I’ve seen a weekend cottage near Dorking that I would like to buy,” continued Miss\nMebbin. “Six hundred and eighty pounds. Good price, but I don’t happen to have the money.”\n\n\n\n         Miss Mebbin named her pretty weekend cottage ‘the Wild Beasts’ and planted a lot of\ntiger-lilies in her garden, and her friends loved it so much. Since then, Mrs Packletide has\nnever gone hunting. When her friends asked Mrs Packletide why she had stopped hunting,\n⑥she answered, “The unplanned costs were just too heavy.”\n\n\n* achievement 「達成、偉業」                     * tiger-claw brooch「虎のつめでつくったブローチ」\n* rupees「ルピー (インドの通貨)」                    * goat「ヤギ」\n* disturb「妨げる」\n                                                 6"}, {"page": 10, "text": "問１ 下線部①detestation の意味として最も適切なものを次の中から一つ選び、記号で答え\n   なさい。\n    ア 嫌悪             イ 無関心   ウ 好意      エ 協力\n\n問２ 下線部②[        ]内に入る最も適切なものを次の中から一つ選び、記号で答えなさい。\n    ア Do you think the tiger will show up?\n    イ Do you think we are in danger?\n    ウ Do you think you can shoot the tiger?\n    エ Do you think the tiger is really old?\n\n問３ 下線部③[        ]内に入る最も適切なものを次の中から一つ選び、記号で答えなさい。\n    ア excited about  イ proud of ウ interested in エ disappointed at\n\n問４ 下線部④[            ]内に入る最も適切な語を本文中から一語で抜き出し、答えなさい。\n\n問５    下線部⑤においてメビンは何をしているのか。最も適切なものを次の中から一つ選び、\n      記号で答えなさい。\n       ア 別荘の良い物件を見つけたので、パックルタイド婦人に購入を勧めている。\n       イ 気に入った別荘を買いたいけれど、十分な資金がないことを嘆いている。\n       ウ 狩りでの秘密をばらさないで欲しければ、別荘を買うお金を払うように脅している。\n       エ 気に入った別荘を購入するために、狩りの手伝いを引き受けようとしている。\n\n問６ 下線部⑥の理由として最も適切なものを次の中から一つ選び、記号で答えなさい。\n    ア Mrs Packletide had to pay more than she had originally planned to the villagers\n      who helped her shoot the tiger.\n    イ Mrs Packletide had to buy a cottage for Miss Mebbin in order to keep her mouth\n      shut.\n    ウ Mrs Packletide became so serious about her hobby of hunting that she spent too\n      much money on hunting tools.\n    エ Mrs Packletide was not planning to go hunting dangerous animals because it\n      might cost her her life.\n\n問７ 本文の内容と一致するものを次の中から二つ選び、記号で答えなさい。\n   ア Mrs Packletide decided to shoot a tiger because she wanted to achieve something\n     bigger than Loona Bimberton.\n   イ Mrs Packletide chose to kill the old tiger in the village because it was the biggest\n     and scariest tiger that people had ever seen.\n   ウ Mothers in the village were so scared of the tiger that they even stopped singing\n     to their babies.\n   エ Miss Mebbin thought that Mrs Packletide paid very little for all the work that the\n     villagers did to help her shoot the tiger.\n   オ Loona Bimberton was very happy to receive a tiger-claw brooch from Mrs\n     Packletide for her birthday.\n   カ Miss Mebbin took advantage of Mrs Packletide’s secret in order to get what she\n     wanted.\n   キ After her successful hunt of the tiger, Mrs Packletide started spending more time\n     hunting wild animals in the jungle.\n                                           7"}, {"page": 11, "text": "余白\n\n\n8"}, {"page": 12, "text": "６     次の英文を読んで、後の問１～８に答えなさい。\n        Over time, the human brain hasn’t changed much. A long time ago, humans focused\non things like hunting or gathering wild plants. Today, we are constantly looking at TVs,\ncomputers, and mobile phones for digital information. Many scientists believe this could be\nharmful to the brain.\n        In October 2020, scientists reported ①a new study in the scientific journal Nature.\nThey studied how media multitasking affects memory. Media multitasking is to use more\nthan one digital device at the same time. ②[        ], some people watch TV while also playing\nvideo games or using social media. The scientists found that this could harm our brain,\nnegatively affecting our attention span and ③[         ].\n        (ア) In this research, 80 young adults between the ages of 18 and 26 joined the\nexperiment. They were told to remember images of certain objects on a computer screen.\nAfter 10 minutes, they looked at more pictures and had to tell the scientists if they had\nalready seen the same images or not.\n        (イ) During this experiment, the scientists measured the brain activity and checked\nthe eye movements of each participant. This allowed them to see when the participants were\npaying attention and when they were not. The scientists compared the results to a survey\nthe participants had answered before the experiment. ④The survey asked questions about\ntheir habits of media multitasking. It also asked about times they experienced attention\nissues and memory problems.\n        (ウ) Such participants stopped paying attention more often during the test. The\nscientists noticed that their pupils, the dark centers of their eyes, got smaller. This was a\nsign that they were no longer paying attention. These participants performed poorly when\nasked to remember the images they had seen.\n        (エ) The scientists found that people who are media multitaskers have more memory\ndifficulties. It could be because they find it hard in many situations to stay focused. When\npeople can pay attention for a longer time, they can remember things better. But if they stop\npaying attention, they have trouble remembering things.\n        The study is a step towards understanding how media affect memory. The scientists\nhope to continue designing more experiments. ⑤They want to find out [ 1 ] [ 2 ] is one type\nof digital device that [ 3 ] [ 4 ] [ 5 ] to the brain than others. They also wish to develop ways\nto help people pay attention for a longer time and remember things better.\n        Meanwhile, you should be careful of how you use digital devices and ⑥ resist\nmultitasking if you would like to have a good memory. So try not to send text messages\nduring school lectures or flip through Instagram while watching a movie.\n                                               9"}, {"page": 13, "text": "問１ 下線部①について、次の空所に入る最も適切なものを次の中から一つ選び、記号で答\n   えなさい。\n   The findings of the study suggest that (  ).\n   ア media multitaskers do not remember things well since they cannot keep focused\n     for a long time\n   イ media multitaskers cannot keep focused for a long time since they do not\n     remember things well\n   ウ media multitaskers can keep focused for a long time since they remember things\n     well\n   エ media multitaskers remember things well since they can keep focused for a long\n     time\n\n問２ 下線部②[            ]に入るものを次の中から一つ選び、記号で答えなさい。\n   ア However         イ As a result ウ In addition エ For example\n\n問３ 下線部③[            ]に入る最も適切な語を本文中から一語で抜き出し、答えなさい。\n\n問４ 下線部④に含まれる質問として、不適切なものを一つ選び、記号で答えなさい。\n   ア Do you feel forgetful when you are at school or work?\n   イ Do you often check your phone messages while watching TV?\n   ウ Do you attend computer lessons on how to use social media?\n   エ Do you often think of other things while you are working on a task?\n\n問５ 下線部⑤について、次の日本語とほぼ同じ内容になるように、空所に入る英語を一語\n   ずつ答えなさい。\n  「彼らは、ある種のデジタル機器が他の機器よりも、より脳に有害かどうかを調べた\n   い。」\n\n問６ 下線部⑥resist の意味に最も近いものを次の中から一つ選び、記号で答えなさい。\n   ア avoid     イ attract ウ show     エ choose\n\n問７ 次の英文を入れるのに最も適切な箇所を本文中の(ア) ～ (エ)の中から一つ選び、記号\n   で答えなさい。\n   In the survey, some participants reported more media multitasking than others.\n\n問８ 本文の内容と一致するものを次の中から一つ選び、記号で答えなさい。\n   ア Throughout history, humans have been doing harm to their brains by gathering\n     wild plants or watching TV.\n   イ In the experiment, participants had to draw the same images as they had seen on\n     the computer screen.\n   ウ The scientists tracked the eye movements of participants to examine if they were\n     focused or not during the test.\n   エ The scientists are trying to develop ways to improve people’s multitasking skills.\n                                          10"}], "2023": [{"page": 4, "text": "３   問１〜４に答えなさい。\n問１ 下線部の発音が他と異なるものを次の中から一つ選び、記号で答えなさい。\n\n\n     ア breathe                 イ spread               ウ weapon                エ feather\n\n\n問２ 第一アクセント(第一強勢)の位置が他と異なるものを次の中から一つ選び、記号で答\n    えなさい。\n\n\n     ア musician                イ character            ウ delicious             エ official\n\n\n問３ 次の(１)、(２)が日本語の意味になるように [                               ] 内の語（句）を並べかえなさ\n    い。解答欄には、[                 ] 内で２番目、５番目になる語（句）を記号で答えなさい。\n\n\n（１）彼は休暇中に犬の世話をしてくれる人を探しています。\n    He is [ア someone / イ care / ウ looking / エ his dog / オ of / カ for / キ who /\n    ク can take ] during his vacation.\n\n\n（２）彼女は英語を一生懸命勉強したので、今では英語の本を読むことができる。\n    She [ア that / イ studied / ウ written / エ can read / オ English / カ she / キ books /\n    ク so hard ] in English now.\n\n\n問４ 次の英文の空所に入る英語を一語ずつ答えなさい。ただし、与えられた文字から始め\n    ること。\n\n\n（１）A (c      ) is a formal event that is performed on important social or religious\n    occasions.\n\n\n（２）To (p         ) is to tell somebody that you will surely do or not do something.\n\n\n                                             3"}, {"page": 5, "text": "４       次の英文を読んで、英文の要約(summary)を５０語以内の英語で書きなさい。\n\n\n        Sam and Mr. Brown were neighbors. Sam was a poor farmer. Mr. Brown was a\nlandlord.\n        Although Sam had no money, he was always very relaxed and happy. He never\nbothered to lock the doors and windows of his house at night. He slept well every night.\n        Mr. Brown was always very stressed. He was careful to lock the doors and windows\nof his house at night. He could not sleep well. He was always worried that someone might\nbreak open his cash boxes and steal away his money. He was jealous of Sam’s peaceful\nlife.\n        One day, Mr. Brown called Sam and gave him a boxful of cash saying, “Look, my dear\nfriend. I am blessed with plenty of wealth. I find you in poverty. So, take this cash and\nlive in wealth.”\n        Sam was extremely happy. He was joyful throughout the day. Night came. Sam went\nto bed as usual. But, that night, he could not sleep. He went and locked all the doors and\nwindows. He still could not sleep. He started watching the box of cash. The whole night\nhe was worried.\n        As soon as the sun rose, Sam took the box of cash to Mr. Brown. He gave back the box\nto Mr. Brown saying, “Dear friend, I am poor. But your money took away peace from me.\nPlease understand this and take back your money.”\n\n\n                                               4"}, {"page": 6, "text": "５     次の英文を読んで、後の問１～７に答えなさい。\n\n       “Imagine there was a wish machine built into your wall. You just walk up to it and\nmake a wish and what you wish for would come out, just like a vending machine,” Ray said.\n       “I’m tired of your stupid invention game,” Benny said to his friend. Ray didn’t listen\nto Benny and drew a machine with the words “MAGICAL WISH MACHINE” on a piece of\npaper and taped it right above the hollowed-out fireplace*. Benny looked at the drawing and\nlaughed. ①“If people are bad at studies, they are usually good at something else. For you,\nthough, that ‘something else’ definitely isn’t art.”\n\n         After Ray went home that night, Benny couldn’t sleep. He was thinking about the\ntest he had to take to get into a good high school, about his basketball tournament and about\nthe girl he liked. But mostly, Benny thought about his sick grandmother, sleeping in the next\nroom. He could hear her coughing at night. What if something happened to his grandmother?\nHe couldn’t even think about it without feeling sick. He got up and decided to go to the\nbathroom. On the way, he saw Ray’s drawing. He smiled and said, “I wish for noise-cancelling\nheadphones that will help me sleep.”\n\n         When Benny woke up the next morning, he found a pair of earplugs on the chair next\nto the fireplace. ②He picked them up and looked at Ray’s drawing and back at the earplugs.\n         ‘This machine cannot be real. Maybe someone is playing a trick on me,’ he said to\nhimself.\n         “Did you place these earplugs on the chair, Grandma?” asked Benny.\n         “No, why would I?” replied his grandmother.\n\n        After school that day, Benny was walking with his friends to a snack shop. A thin\nhomeless man, who was sitting at his usual spot, said to them, “Give me some coins.” Benny’s\nfriends kept walking without even looking at the man. Benny stopped and gave him some\ncoins as he always did. He felt sorry for the homeless man.\n\n        When Benny came home that night, his grandmother was already in bed. He opened\nthe fridge but it was empty. He stood in front of the wish machine and said “I wish for a\nlarge-sized meat pizza.” He waited there for a while but nothing happened. “There is no way\nthat the wish machine is ③legitimate,” Benny laughed and went to have a shower. When he\ncame out of the bathroom, he found a brown box on the chair. He opened the box and found\ntwo small pieces of mushroom pizza. He looked around the room, thinking that there may be\na hidden camera. There was nothing. Benny sat on the sofa and ate the pizza.\n\n        When Benny came home from school the next day, his grandmother was watching\nTV on the sofa. Benny joined her, and they started talking.\n        “The building next door has been empty for many years. Who owns it?” Benny asked.\n        “Some old woman who lives in an apartment in Greenpoint. She doesn’t want to sell\nthe building.”\n        “How much do you think she could sell the building for? Millions?”\n        “Why do I care? I’m not interested.”\n                                             5"}, {"page": 7, "text": "         “If you had that kind of money, what is the first thing you would buy, Grandma?”\n         “I wouldn’t buy anything, but I would hire a detective* to help me find something I\nlost.”\n        “What did you lose?” Benny asked, knowing that she meant the gold necklace from\nhis grandfather. It was the last present that his grandfather gave to his wife for their\nanniversary. Benny waited for her answer, but she just smiled, turned off the TV and went\nto bed. Benny walked up to the wish machine and said, “I wish for the gold necklace that my\ngrandmother lost.”\n\n        The next morning, Benny was ④[          ] to find nothing on the chair. ‘Maybe a special\nwish like this takes more time,’ he said to himself. Then, Benny checked on his grandmother,\nwho was still in bed.\n        “Are you okay, Grandma? You don’t look well today. I should take you to the doctor.”\n        “No, you should go to school. Now hurry up and go!” his grandmother said.\n\n        At school, Benny couldn’t focus on his studies as he was worried about his\ngrandmother. As soon as class was over, he hurried home. When he arrived at his apartment\nbuilding, his body froze. There were police cars and an ambulance. He dropped his school bag\nand tried to run into the building. A police officer stopped him.\n        “What happened to my Grandma?” Benny’s voice was shaking.\n        “Slow down, son,” the police officer said.\n        Then Benny saw people carrying a stretcher* out of the building. “No! Please!” Benny\nfell down on his knees.\n        “It’s going to be okay, son,” the officer said.\n        “Is she going to live?” Benny asked.\n        “Yes, she is,” the officer said. “Thanks to that man.” He pointed at a man in\nhandcuffs*. Benny realized who it was. He was the ⑤[          ] whom Benny always gave coins\nto.\n        “Did he hurt my Grandma?” Benny asked the police officer.\n        “No, you got it wrong, son. The man has been staying in the empty building next door\nfor a few weeks now, and he went into your apartment from the connecting fireplace. Then\nhe found your grandmother on the floor and called the ambulance. That man saved your\ngrandmother’s life.”\n        “⑥[       ]” asked Benny.\n        “That man was holding a silver necklace in his hand. He probably stole it from your\ngrandmother,” said the police officer.\n        As the man was led to the police car, he said to Benny, “I just wanted you to believe.”\n        Then Benny understood everything. The man was listening to Benny’s wishes\nthrough the connecting fireplace.\n        “No, the man didn’t steal it from my Grandma. He was trying to put it in our\napartment.”\n       Now the police officer looked confused. Benny realized he had a lot of things to explain.\n\n         *hollowed-out fireplace 「壁にくり抜かれた暖炉」                   *detective 「探偵」\n         *stretcher              「担架」                           *handcuffs「手錠」\n                                               6"}, {"page": 8, "text": "問１ ベニーが下線部①のように言った理由として最も適切なものを次の中から一つ選び、記\n   号で答えなさい。\n   ア Ray always came up with stupid invention games.\n      イ Ray’s drawing of the wish machine was not good.\n      ウ Ray did not perform well at school.\n      エ Ray was usually good at most things.\n\n問２ 下線部②が表している状況として最も適切なものを次の中から一つ選び、記号で答えな\n   さい。\n   ア ウィッシュ・マシーンが雑音消去ヘッドホンの代わりに耳栓をくれたのかどうか、\n     疑問に思っている様子。\n      イ ウィッシュ・マシーンに雑音消去ヘッドホンの代わりに耳栓をもらえて、感謝して\n        いる様子。\n      ウ ウィッシュ・マシーンに雑音消去ヘッドホンの代わりに耳栓を渡され、怒っている\n        様子。\n      エ ウィッシュ・マシーンが雑音消去ヘッドホンの代わりに耳栓をくれたと知り、困っ\n        ている様子。\n\n\n問３ 下線部③legitimate の意味に最も近いものを次の中から一つ選び、記号で答えなさい。\n   ア broken         イ interesting ウ empty エ real\n\n問４ 下線部④[            ]内に入る最も適切なものを次の中から一つ選び、記号で答えなさい。\n   アexcited              イembarrassed ウdisappointed エpleased\n\n問５ 下線部⑤[            ]内に入る最も適切な語句を本文中から二語で抜き出し、答えなさい。\n\n問６ 下線部⑥[       ]内に入る最も適切なものを次の中から一つ選び、記号で答えなさい。\n   ア Why did he save my grandmother’s life?\n   イ Why was he arrested then?\n   ウ How did he break into our apartment?\n   エ How long was he staying in the empty building?\n\n問７ 本文の内容と一致するものを次の中から二つ選び、記号で答えなさい。\n   ア Benny asked Ray to draw a picture of a wish machine because he had many wishes\n     to make.\n   イ Benny and his friends always helped the homeless man by giving him coins.\n   ウ The pizza Benny received from the wish machine was exactly what he had asked\n     for.\n   エ Benny’s grandmother wished for a lot of money because she wanted to buy a gold\n     necklace.\n   オ The first two things Benny asked for were given to him in a different form.\n   カ The police officer told Benny that his grandmother would not be able to survive.\n   キ The homeless man was trying to make Benny’s wishes come true.\n\n\n                                              7"}, {"page": 9, "text": "余白\n\n\n\n8"}, {"page": 10, "text": "６     次の英文を読んで、後の問１～８に答えなさい。\n\n       Science experiments do not always go as planned, but that does not mean you\ncannot learn from them. Recently, Australian scientists learned something unexpected\nabout magpies when they tried to put trackers* on the birds to learn more about their\nhabits.\n       The birds the scientists were studying were called Australian magpies. They are\nmedium-sized black and white birds from the same bird “family” as crows. They are\nknown to be quite clever. They live in groups of up to 12 birds and work together to\nprotect the area they live in.\n       (ア) The researchers hoped to learn more about how far the birds flew and what\nthey did during a normal day. ①The scientists were [ 1 ] that magpies might [ 2 ]\na hard [ 3 ] living in a [ 4 ] environment caused by climate change.\n       (イ) However, it was hard to find trackers that worked well with medium-sized\nbirds. Either the trackers were too heavy, or did not last long enough. Still, the\nresearchers managed to find new trackers that were light and could be recharged\nwirelessly while they were still on the birds.\n       The researchers also developed a special harness* for the tracker. The harness\nwas strong and did not come off easily but had a special release controlled by magnets.\n②[       ] this magnetic release, the scientists could free the birds from the harnesses\nwithout catching the birds again.\n       There was just one problem – the birds didn’t like the harnesses with the trackers.\nThe researchers placed trackers on five magpies using their special harnesses. But just\nten minutes after finishing up with the last magpie, they found something unusual. An\nadult female magpie was ③[          ] out another magpie in getting free from its harness.\n       (ウ) In a few hours, most of the other magpies had also been freed from their\nharnesses. By the third day, none of the birds had their harnesses with trackers anymore.\n“The birds ④outsmarted us,” said Dominique Potvin, one of the scientists.\n       (エ) The scientists were disappointed about the trackers, but they realized that the\nmagpies had taught them something else; magpies are able and happy to help each other\nout, even if the bird who is helping does not get a reward.\n       This is called “rescue behavior”, and it is not common in the animal world,\nespecially among birds. The researchers think this is the first time rescue behavior has\nbeen reported for Australian magpies.\n       The scientists are guessing that the magpies thought the trackers were small bugs.\nHowever, they are still not sure if just one magpie solved ⑤the difficult problem or\nseveral worked together.\n       The researchers are glad they have learned about the rescue behavior of magpies.\nBut now they need to figure out a good way to track these birds and try again to complete\n⑥their original research.\n\n      *tracker 追跡用の装置                 *harness 装置をつけるためのベルト\n\n                                            9"}, {"page": 11, "text": "問１ 下線部①について、次の日本語とほぼ同じ内容になるように、空所に入る英語を一語ずつ答\n   えなさい。\n   「気候変動が理由で、より暖かくなってきた環境の中で、マグパイが生きていくのに苦労をす\n    るかもしれないことを科学者たちは心配していた。」\n\n問２ 下線部②[           ]に入るものを次の中から一つ選び、記号で答えなさい。\n   ア Up to           イ Instead of ウ Thanks to エ Far from\n\n問３ 下線部③[           ]に入る最も適切な語を本文中から一語で抜き出し、答えなさい。\n\n問４ 下線部④outsmarted us の意味に最も近いものを次の中から一つ選び、記号で答えなさい。\n   ア found out that we were smart\n   イ did better than us by using their intelligence\n   ウ showed they were not as intelligent as us\n   エ cried out to us because they were smart\n\n問５ 下線部⑤の内容として最も適切なものを次の中から一つ選び、記号で答えなさい。\n   ア finding small bugs on the trackers\n   イ working together to learn something new\n   ウ taking the harnesses off from their bodies\n   エ flying with the tracker on their bodies\n\n問６ 下線部⑥の内容として最も適切なものを次の中から一つ選び、記号で答えなさい。\n   ア how the birds behave on regular days\n   イ how smart the birds are\n   ウ how the birds learn to help others\n   エ how to make good trackers for the birds\n\n問７ 次の英文を入れるのに最も適切な箇所を本文中の(ア) ～ (エ)の中から一つ選び、記号で\n   答えなさい。\n   The researchers needed to use newer and better trackers on the magpies to study\n   their habits.\n\n問８ 本文の内容と一致するものを次の中から一つ選び、記号で答えなさい。\n   ア Magpies did not mind wearing the special harness with a tracker as it was light.\n   イ As the magpies were strong and clever, they carried heavy trackers easily.\n   ウ Rescue behavior is not often found in the animal world, especially among birds.\n   エ The magpies knew that they would be given small bugs if they helped others.\n\n\n                                         10"}], "2024": [{"page": 4, "text": "３   問１〜４に答えなさい。\n問１ 下線部の発音が他と異なるものを次の中から一つ選び、記号で答えなさい。\n\n\n     ア trouble                   イ thousand         ウ enough                エ touch\n\n\n問２ 第一アクセント(第一強勢)の位置が他と異なるものを次の中から一つ選び、記号で答\n     えなさい。\n\n\n     ア continue                  イ passenger        ウ accident              エ finally\n\n\n問３ 次の(１)、(２)が日本語の意味になるように [                             ] 内の語（句）を並べかえなさ\n     い。解答欄には、[               ] 内で２番目、５番目になる語（句）を記号で答えなさい。\n\n\n（１）学園祭の準備をみんなとても頑張りました。\n     Everybody [ア of / イ preparing / ウ a lot / エ put / オ into / カ for / キ effort\n     / ク the school festival].\n\n\n（２）その情報の出元を調べた方がいいですね。あまりにも聞こえが良すぎます。\n     We should find [ア as / イ comes / ウ out / エ the information / オ where\n     / カ it sounds / キ from] too good to be true.\n\n\n問４ 次の英文の空所に入る英語を一語ずつ答えなさい。ただし、与えられた文字から始め\n     ること。\n\n\n（１）If you (w        ) something such as time, money, or energy, you use too much of it\n     doing something that is not important or necessary.\n\n\n（２）(M        ) is something that you drink or swallow in order to cure an illness.\n\n\n                                               3"}, {"page": 5, "text": "４      以下の会話を読み、会話文の A さんの空所に、B さんの意見をまとめた要約と、それ\n     に対する反論を英語で書きなさい。語数は６０語以内とする。\n\n\n\nA: What do you think about allowing the use of mobile phones in schools?\n\nB: That’s a difficult question. If mobile phones are allowed in schools, there might be\n    cases where mobile phones ring or students exchange messages during lessons. If\n    such things happen, it would be difficult to concentrate on lessons. Besides, I’m\n    afraid students might start playing mobile games during break time. The school\n    will lose its academic atmosphere. Personally, I’m against it.\n\nA: （                                    ）\n\nB: I see. That might work. The key to solving the problem might be to set up some\n    rules on the use of them, like the one you mentioned. Can you come up with\n    another one?\n\n\n                                             4"}, {"page": 6, "text": "５    次の英文を読んで、後の問１～６に答えなさい。\n         Yollie’s mother, Mrs. Moreno, was a large woman with a kind heart. Even though\nshe was a single mother and didn’t have much money to raise her daughter by herself,\nshe was always cheerful and making everybody laugh.\n         Mrs. Moreno encouraged Yollie to study hard because she wanted her daughter\nto be a doctor. She bought Yollie a desk and a lamp so that she could study at night.\n         Yollie was a slender, pretty girl, who was the smartest in her school. Everything\nwas good at school except for one problem; Yollie needed a new dress for her school dance.\nHer mother couldn’t afford one. It was late October, with Christmas around the corner,\nand her mother had gotten her car repaired recently, which cost her almost one hundred\ndollars.\n         “We don’t have the money,” said her mother, feeling sad because she couldn’t buy\na new dress, even though there was a little money saved for college.\n         “We can color your white summer dress so it will look like a brand-new evening\ndress,” Mrs. Moreno said brightly. She poured hot water and black dye* into a bucket\nand stirred it with a pencil. Then, slowly and carefully, she lowered the dress into the\nbucket.\n         ①Yollie found it difficult to watch, so she decided to leave her mother alone. She\ndidn’t want to see her mother’s disappointed face as she knew that the whole thing would\nend up a terrible failure. However, to Yollie’s surprise, the dress came out shiny and black.\nIt looked brand-new and was beautiful. She smiled at her mother. Then her mother\nhugged Yollie and said, “See, what did I tell you?”\n         The dance was important to Yollie because she was in love with a boy called Ernie\nCastillo. After she dressed and put on makeup, Mrs. Moreno drove Yollie to school, and\nYollie ran to meet her best friend, Janice.\n         The evening was warm but thick with clouds. The strong wind made the paper\nlanterns in the trees swing, coloring the night with reds and yellows. The lanterns made\nthe evening feel romantic, like a movie. Everyone danced, drank fruit punch, and stood\nin groups talking. Ernie, who was standing nearby, was looking at Yollie. He ate some\ncookies nervously and then asked her for a dance.\n         “Sure,” she said, and they danced together. As they circled under the lanterns,\nrain began falling, lightly at first. She leaned her head on Ernie’s shoulder. He felt warm\nand tender. Yollie could tell that he was in love with her. The dance continued\nromantically until it began to rain heavily.\n         “Everyone, let’s go inside — and, boys, carry the table and the record player in,”\na teacher shouted.\n         The girls and boys ran into the cafeteria. Inside, the girls hurried to the restrooms\nto brush their hair and dry themselves. Yollie went to a mirror. She looked a little gray\nnow as her makeup had made black lines on her face. She combed her hair, careful not\nto pull too hard. She couldn’t wait to get back to Ernie.\n                                              5"}, {"page": 7, "text": "        Yollie bent over to pick up a pin, and ②her face turned pale. A black puddle* was\nforming at her feet. The dye was running from her dress like black tears. Yollie stood up.\nHer dress was now the color of ash*. She looked around the room. The other girls did not\nnotice Yollie’s problem. She was worried everyone would laugh if they found out that she\nhad dyed an old dress because she couldn’t afford a new one. She hurried from the\nrestroom with her head down, across the cafeteria floor and out the door. She raced\nthrough the storm, crying as the rain mixed with her tears.\n        When she arrived home, her mother was on the couch eating cookies and\nwatching TV.\n        “How was the dance, baby?” she asked.\n        Yollie ran to her bedroom, undressed and threw the dress on the floor. Her mother\ncame into the room.\n        “What’s going on?”\n        “The dress. It’s cheap! It’s no good!” Yollie kicked the dress at her mother. Mrs.\nMoreno picked it up and looked at it closely but couldn’t see what was wrong.\n        “What’s the matter? It’s just wet.”\n        “The dye came out.” Yollie said angrily.\n        Mrs. Moreno looked at her hands and saw that the black dye was in her palms.\nShe wanted to tell her daughter how sorry she was, but she knew it wouldn’t help. She\nwalked back to the living room and cried.\n        The next morning, the mother and daughter stayed away from each other. Yollie\nsat in her room turning the pages of an old magazine, while her mother watered her\nplants.\n        Yollie was ③[         ] about what had happened the night before. She knew that\nit wasn’t her mother’s fault that they were poor. When they sat down together for lunch,\nthe phone rang. Yollie answered it, thinking it would be Janice wanting to know why she\nhad left. But it was Ernie wondering the same thing. When he found out she wasn’t angry\nat him, he asked if she would like to go to a movie.\n        “④ [        ],” Yollie said, smiling. She covered the phone with her hand and\ncounted to ten. Then she uncovered the phone and said, “She says it’s OK. What are we\ngoing to see?”\n        After Yollie hung up, her mother climbed onto a chair to reach the top shelf in\nthe hall closet. She wondered why she hadn’t done it earlier. She reached behind a stack\nof towels and pushed her hand into a little box where she kept her secret money.\n       “I’ve been saving a little every month,” said Mrs. Moreno, “for you to go to college.”\nShe held up five twenty dollar notes. They drove to a shop and bought a blouse, shoes,\nand a skirt that would not lose its color in ⑤[       ] or any other kind of weather.\n\n\n*dye「染料」                *puddle「水たまり」                   *ash「灰」\n\n                                             6"}, {"page": 8, "text": "問１ 下線部①が表している状況として最も適切なものを次の中から一つ選び、記号で答え\n   なさい。\n   ア 母親がダンスパーティに着ていくドレスを買ってくれないので、怒っている様子。\n   イ 母親がなぜドレスを黒く染めようとしているのか理解できない様子。\n   ウ 母親が失敗すると思ったので、ドレスを染めるのを見ていられない様子。\n   エ 母親にはドレスを染めるのが難しいと思ったので、自分でやろうとしている様子。\n\n問２ 下線部②の理由として最も適切なものを次の中から一つ選び、記号で答えなさい。\n   ア She noticed that her makeup had made black lines on her face.\n    イ She realized that the black dye was running from her dress.\n    ウ The other girls knew that her family was too poor to buy a new dress.\n    エ The other girls laughed at her because black water was dripping from her dress.\n\n\n問３ 下線部③[           ]内に入る最も適切なものを次の中から一つ選び、記号で答えなさい。\n   ア excited            イ embarrassed ウ pleased エ bored\n\n問４ 下線部④[           ]内に入る最も適切なものを次の中から一つ選び、記号で答えなさい。\n    ア I’m sorry that I left the dance early\n    イ I don’t like to go to the movies\n    ウ I’ll ask my mom\n    エ I wasn’t angry at you\n\n問５ 下線部⑤[           ]内に入る最も適切な語を本文中から一語で抜き出し、答えなさい。\n\n\n問６ 本文の内容と一致するものを次の中から一つ選び、記号で答えなさい。\n    ア Yollie was disappointed that she didn’t have a chance to dance with Ernie as\n       everyone went home after it started raining.\n    イ After the dance, Mrs. Moreno was angry because her daughter complained about\n       the dress she had dyed.\n    ウ Yollie’s best friend Janice called to ask why Yollie had left the dance early.\n    エ After Ernie asked Yollie out for a movie, Mrs. Moreno decided to spend the\n       money she had saved so that she could make her daughter happy.\n\n\n                                           7"}, {"page": 9, "text": "余白\n\n\n8"}, {"page": 10, "text": "６     次の英文を読んで、後の問１～６に答えなさい。\n\n        Many animals like to play just for fun. Cats, dogs, and birds enjoy playing. But\nwhat about insects? Can they also play and have fun? New research says that\n①bumblebees might enjoy rolling wooden balls without any training or rewards. The\nresearchers found that bees not only respond to rewards but also do activities that might\nbring them joy. This shows that bees are more complex than we thought. If they play just\nfor fun, it makes us wonder about their emotions and how aware they are of their\nsurroundings.\n        (ア) In a previous study, researchers trained bees to play soccer with wooden balls\nand gave the bees rewards for scoring goals. During that study, some bees were seen\nplaying with the balls for no apparent reason.\n        (イ) In this study, 45 bees commonly found in Europe were put in a container\nthat had one area with food and another separate area with wooden balls. Then the bees\nhad a choice. They could take the direct path to the feeding place or go to the space where\nthey could play with the balls. Surprisingly, the bees liked the area with the ②[           ]\nmore. The researchers noticed the bees were rolling them with their bodies. Some bees\nonly rolled a ball once, while others did it many times. One bee rolled a ball 117 times!\n        (ウ) The fact that the bees kept coming back to roll the balls shows that they\nfound it enjoyable. Male bees played with the balls longer than females did, and younger\nbees played more than the older ones. ③This is similar to what we see in other animals\nwhen they play.\n        (エ) The behavior of the bees observed in the study is scientifically considered as\n“play” as it was voluntary and rewarding in itself. The bees did not try to get sugary\nrewards by rolling the balls. They knew they could get nectar in a different place. Their\nbehavior was not for food but for fun. In fact, some of the bees came back again and again\nto play with the balls.\n      This research suggests that bees can play. However, ④scientists are ( 1 ) not\n( 2 ) ( 3 ) bees play and how playing ( 4 ) them. If bees play just for fun, it could\nmean that they have feelings and that they are more aware of their surroundings than\nwe realize.\n\n\n                                            9"}, {"page": 11, "text": "問１ 下線部①にある“rewards”の具体例として最も適切なものを次の中から一つ選び、記号で\n  答えなさい。\n  ア joy             イ food                   ウ balls               エ bees\n\n\n問２ 下線部②[          ]に入る最も適切な語を本文中から一語で抜き出し、答えなさい。\n\n\n問３ 下線部③の内容として最も適切なものを次の中から一つ選び、記号で答えなさい。\n   ア It is common for male and young animals to get lost in play for a longer period\n      of time than female and older ones.\n   イ It is common for male and young animals to lose interest in play when they play\n      for a longer period of time than female and older ones.\n   ウ It is important for male and young animals to play with other animals for a\n      longer period of time than female and older ones.\n   エ It is important for male and young animals to be interested in other animals for\n      a longer period of time than female and older ones.\n\n\n問４ 下線部④について、次の日本語とほぼ同じ内容になるように、空所に入る英語を一語\n   ずつ答えなさい。\n  「科学者たちは、なぜハチが遊ぶのか、遊びが何の役に立つのかはいまだよくわかっ\n   ていない。」\n\n\n問５ 次の英文を入れるのに最も適切な箇所を本文中の(ア) ～ (エ)の中から一つ選び、記号で\n  答えなさい。\n  To investigate further, the researchers conducted a new experiment.\n\n\n問６ 本文の内容と一致するものを次の中から一つ選び、記号で答えなさい。\n  ア Most of the bees in the new study chose to go to an area where they could find\n     food.\n  イ The new study showed that the bees seemed to be having fun rolling balls.\n  ウ Even though the bees liked playing with balls, it was not scientifically considered\n     as play.\n  エ Many bees in the new study tried to get nectar by playing with the balls.\n\n\n                                        10"}], "2025": [{"page": 6, "text": "３   問１〜４に答えなさい。\n問１ 下線部太字“g”の発音が他と異なるものを次の中から一つ選び、記号で答えなさい。\n\n\n     ア gesture               イ globally               ウ greet               エ gate\n\n\n問２ 第一アクセント(第一強勢)の位置が他と異なるものを次の中から一つ選び、記号で答\n     えなさい。\n\n\n     ア problem                イ reduce               ウ climate              エ weather\n\n\n問３ 次の(１)、(２)が日本語の意味になるように [                               ] 内の語（句）を並べかえなさ\n     い。解答欄には、[               ] 内で２番目、５番目になる語（句）を記号で答えなさい。\n\n\n（１）ジャックは先週にはレポートを完成させていたが、病気になってしまったため提出で\n     きなかった。\n     Jack finished his report last week, [ア he fell / イ it / ウ because / エ he couldn’t /\n     オ hand / カ in / キ but] ill.\n\n\n（２）そのイベントに出席した人はみんな、そのドキドキするようなパフォーマンスに驚い\n     た。\n     Those [ア by / イ were all / ウ the / エ exciting / オ attended / カ who / キ amazed /\n     ク the event] performances.\n\n\n\n問４ 次の英文の空所に入る英語を一語ずつ答えなさい。ただし、与えられた文字から始め\n     ること。\n\n\n（１）Something that is (p        ) is enjoyed or liked by a lot of people.\n\n\n（２）To (s       ) something means to find an answer to a problem.\n\n\n                                            3"}, {"page": 7, "text": "４ 以下の会話を読み、会話文の Ken の空所に、Hana の意見をまとめた要約と、それに\n   対する反論を英語で書きなさい。語数は 50 語程度とする。\n\n\n\nKen : I went to the zoo with my family the other day, and it was really fun!\n\nHana : Actually, I’ve been thinking about zoos. Some animals in zoos are often taken\n      from faraway places like Africa. Even if they want to move around freely, they are\n      kept in cages. I feel sorry for the animals in that kind of environment, so I think\n      it’s better not to have zoos.\n\nKen : (                        )\n\nHana: You have a point. I didn’t realize that zoos have the benefit you mentioned.\n      Maybe we should think about how to make better use of zoos rather than\n      closing them down.\n\n\n                                           4"}, {"page": 8, "text": "５    次の英文を読んで、後の問１～６に答えなさい。\n         Bill and I needed $2,000 to start a new project in another town, but we were short\non cash. Then we came up with a great idea: kidnapping for ransom*. We thought the\nquiet town called Summit would be the perfect place for our plan.\n         We chose our target: a ten-year-old boy named Johnny Dorset. He was the only\nchild of Ebenezer Dorset, the richest man in the town. We thought that he would pay a\nlarge amount of money quickly to get his son back safely.\n         About 3 kilometers away from Summit, there was a small mountain. On the back\nside of this mountain, there was a cave*. We kept our supplies in that cave.\n         One evening, we found Johnny on the street. He was throwing rocks at a cat.\n         “Hey, little boy! Do you want some candy and a ride in a car?” said Bill.\n         The boy threw a rock at Bill and fought back like a wild bear, but we finally got\nhim into our car. We drove him to our cave. After dark, I drove the car to the town where\nwe rented it, and then walked back to the cave.\n         When I returned, the boy was standing by the cave entrance with bird feathers\nin his hair.\n         “Ha! You white man, do you dare to come into the camp of Red Chief*?” he said.\n         “It’s ok, Sam,” Bill said. “We’re just playing Indian. I’m Old Hank, Red Chief ’s\ncaptive*, who is going to get his head cut off at sunrise. My goodness! That kid can kick\nhard.”\n         The boy seemed to be having ➀[           ]. He called me Snake-eye, the white men’s\nspy who was going to be burned at sunrise.\n         At dinner, the boy talked nonstop. He asked us one question after another. Every\nfew minutes he remembered he was Red Chief and picked up his stick rifle and shouted\nvery loudly, which really scared Bill.\n         “Would you like to go home?” I asked the kid.\n         “Why? I don’t have any fun at home. I like to camp out. You won’t take me back\nhome, will you?”\n         “Not right away,” I said. “We’ll stay here for a while.”\n         “Good!” he said. “I’ve never had such fun in my life.”\n         We went to bed around eleven o’clock. But the boy kept us awake, jumping up\nand reaching for his toy rifle, shouting “Hey! Wake up!” in our ears whenever he heard a\nnoise. Eventually, I fell asleep.\n         Early in the morning, I woke up to Bill’s screams. I jumped up to see what was\nwrong. Red Chief was sitting on Bill, pulling his hair with one hand. In the other hand,\nhe had a knife and was trying to cut off Bill’s head.\n                                              5"}, {"page": 9, "text": "        I took the knife from the kid and made him lie down again. Bill was terrified. I\ntried to go back to sleep but then remembered Red Chief had said I was going to be\nburned at sunrise. I sat up and leaned against a rock.\n        “Why are you up so early, Sam?” asked Bill.\n        “Oh, I have a pain in my shoulder. I thought sitting up would help,” I said.\n        “You’re lying!” said Bill. “You’re ➁[       ]. You were going to be burned at sunrise,\nand you know he will do it. He is terrible. Do you think anyone would pay money to get\na kid like that back home?”\n        “Sure,” I said. “Parents love their kids. We need to send a message to his father\nasking for $2,000.”\n        “Can we make the ransom $1,500 instead of $2,000 so that Mr. Dorset will agree\nquickly?” Bill asked me. I agreed and wrote the letter.\n\n\nDear Mr. Ebenezer Dorset,\n\n\nWe have your boy. The only way you can get him back is by paying us $1,500. Send a\nmessenger to deliver your answer in writing to a box under the big tree at Owl Creek by\n8 o’clock tonight.\n                                                                  TWO TROUBLED MEN\n\n\n\n        I took the letter to the post office. When I got back to the cave, I couldn’t find Bill\nor the boy. In about three hours, Bill came back. Behind him was the kid, stepping softly\nwith a big smile on his face. When Bill stopped, the kid also stopped behind him.\n        “The boy is gone,” Bill said.\n        “What happened?” I asked.\n        “I was ridden,” said Bill, “by the kid as a horse for hours without stopping. I\ncouldn’t take any more, so I carried the boy down the mountain. I showed him the road\nto Summit and let him go. I’m sorry we lost the ransom, but I had no choice.”\nBill was breathing heavily but seemed relieved.\n        “Do you have a heart problem?” I asked.\n        “No, why?” said Bill.\n        “Then you might want to turn around,” I said. ➂Bill turned and saw the boy, and\nthen sat down on the ground. He couldn’t stand up for an hour.\n\n\n                                                6"}, {"page": 10, "text": "Later in the afternoon, I went to Owl Creek and found a note in the box. I took it back\nand opened it. It said:\n\n\nDear Two Troubled Men,\n\n\nI got your letter about the ransom for my son. I also have an offer. Bring Johnny home\nand pay me $250. Then I’ll take him off your hands.\n                                                               EBENEZER DORSET\n\n\n        “How rude!” I said. Then I looked at Bill’s face and stopped.\n        “Sam,” said Bill, “we have the money. One more night with this kid will kill me.\nBesides, Mr. Dorset is very generous. ➃[        ]”\n        “Ok. We’ll take him home and pay the money,” I agreed.\n        That night, we went to Mr. Dorset’s house and handed him the money. When the\nkid found out what was happening, ➄he started shouting and held tightly to Bill’s leg.\nHis father slowly pulled him off.\n        “How long can you hold him?” asked Bill.\n        “I think I can manage ten minutes,” said Mr. Dorset.\n        We started running as fast as we could to get out of Summit.\n\n\n*ransom      「身代金」\n*cave        「洞窟」\n*Red Chief 「レッド・チーフ(少年が自分に付けたネイティブ・インディアン部族の長\n              の名前)」\n*captive     「捕虜」\n\n\n                                            7"}, {"page": 11, "text": "問１ 下線部➀[         ]内に入る最も適切な語を本文中から一語で抜き出し、答えなさい。\n\n\n問２ 下線部➁[         ]内に入る最も適切なものを次の中から一つ選び、記号で答えなさい。\n  アtired                   イscared                ウsurprised           エpleased\n\n\n問３ 下線部➂が表している状況として最も適切なものを次の中から一つ選び、記号で答え\n  なさい。\n  ア 身代金をもらうチャンスを失ってしまい、落ち込んでいる様子。\n  イ 身代金を要求する手紙を出したので、返事を楽しみにしている様子。\n  ウ 誘拐した子供が無事に戻ってきてくれて、安心している様子。\n  エ 誘拐した子供から逃れることができず、絶望している様子。\n\n\n問４ 下線部➃[         ]内に入る最も適切なものを次の中から一つ選び、記号で答えなさい。\n  ア You’re going to request $250, aren’t you?\n  イ You’re taking him home without receiving money, aren’t you?\n  ウ You’re not going to say no to him, are you?\n  エ You’re not going to agree to pay him $250, are you?\n\n\n問５ 下線部➄の理由として最も適切なものを次の中から一つ選び、記号で答えなさい。\n  ア He was upset as he wanted to spend more time with Sam and Bill.\n  イ He was angry as his father didn’t agree to pay the ransom.\n  ウ He was relieved that he was finally able to come home.\n  エ He was so tired that he wanted to go to bed as soon as possible.\n\n\n問６ 本文の内容と一致するものを次の中から一つ選び、記号で答えなさい。\n  ア Sam and Bill were able to get Johnny into their car very quickly without any\n     trouble.\n  イ Sam and Bill asked Mr. Dorset for $2,000 in their ransom letter as they were\n     sure that Mr. Dorset would agree.\n  ウ Sam and Bill felt sad to bring Johnny back home as they enjoyed playing with\n     him.\n  エ Sam and Bill agreed to pay money to Mr. Dorset as they wanted to be free from\n     Johnny.\n\n\n                                         8"}, {"page": 12, "text": "６    次の英文を読んで、後の問１～６に答えなさい。\n      School uniforms might be stopping primary school children, especially girls, from\nbeing ➀[         ], a new study from the University of Cambridge suggests. In countries\nwhere most schools have uniforms, fewer young people meet the World Health\nOrganization’s advice of getting 60 minutes of physical activity a day, according to the\nstudy.\n      The researchers of the study looked at existing data about the physical activity of\nmore than 1 million children aged 5 to 17 years old from 135 countries. They also\nconducted their own online survey to find how common school uniforms were in these\nplaces and how uniforms might affect physical activity.\n      The study found a gender gap. ➁Activity levels were much lower for girls than boys\nin primary schools with uniforms. However, this gap was not seen in older children at\nsecondary schools. This might be because younger children are active all day, including\nduring breaks. On the other hand, older children usually exercise during PE lessons or\nby joining sports teams. When they exercise, they change clothes, so school uniforms do\nnot affect their activity levels as much.\n      （ア）The study also found that girls feel less comfortable being active if they have to\nwear clothes like skirts or dresses. Dr Mairead Ryan, a researcher at Cambridge, said,\n“Schools prefer uniforms for many reasons. We are not saying that schools should stop\nusing them entirely, but ➂we want to share this information (      1 ) ( 2 ) schools can\n(   3 ) ( 4 ) choices. Schools might think about how uniforms are designed to help\nkids stay active during the day.”\n      (イ)However, it supports other studies that suggest uniforms can be restrictive,\nespecially for girls. For example, a study in England showed that the design of girls’\nuniforms stopped some girls from joining in certain activities. Another study in Chile\nshowed that children were more active when they wore sport-friendly uniforms instead\nof traditional clothes like skirts and blazers.\n      (ウ)Another researcher said, “Girls might feel less confident about doing things like\ncartwheels* or riding a bike in a skirt or dress. Social expectations may be part of the\nreason. If it is not considered normal in society for girls to do active sports like running\nor playing games in skirts or dresses, they might avoid these activities when wearing\nsuch clothes.”\n      （エ）Sarah Hannafin, head of policy at the school leaders’ union, said, “Physical\nactivity and sports are key parts in school. Schools should make sure all kids are active.\nThey should remove any ➃barriers to participation.”\n      *cartwheels 「側転」\n                                              9"}, {"page": 13, "text": "問１ 下線部➀[         ]に入る最も適切な語を本文中から一語で抜き出し、答えなさい。\n\n\n問２ 下線部➁の内容として最も適切なものを次の中から一つ選び、記号で答えなさい。\n  ア In primary schools, girls were much less active while wearing uniforms than\n    when not wearing them.\n  イ In primary schools, girls did not like to wear uniforms as much as boys did when\n    their activity levels were low.\n  ウ Girls in primary schools did not like wearing uniforms as much as boys did when\n    they joined school activities.\n  エ Girls were much less active than boys in primary schools that had a uniform\n    policy.\n\n\n問３ 下線部➂について、次の日本語とほぼ同じ内容になるように、空所に入る英語を一語ずつ答\n   えなさい。\n  「私たちは学校がより良い選択ができるように、この情報を共有したいと考えている。」\n\n\n問４ 下線部➃barriers の具体例として本文中にあるものを次の中から一つ選び、記号で答えな\n  さい。\n  ア sports          イ schools                 ウ uniforms            エ kids\n\n\n問５ 次の英文を入れるのに最も適切な箇所を本文中の(ア) ～ (エ)の中から一つ選び、記号で\n  答えなさい。\n  The research did not find uniforms to be the only factor for less physical activity.\n\n\n問６ 本文の内容と一致するものを次の中から一つ選び、記号で答えなさい。\n  ア The study suggests school children become more confident when they wear school\n    uniforms.\n  イ The researchers were trying to find out children’s preferences towards school\n    uniforms through an online survey.\n  ウ Older children do more exercise during PE classes because they can join school\n    sports teams if they work hard in these classes.\n  エ A researcher believes that social expectations may influence how children behave\n    in certain clothes.\n\n\n\n                                         10"}], "2026": [{"page": 6, "text": "３    問１、問２に答えなさい。\n\n問１   下線部の発音が他と異なるものを次の中から一つ選び、記号で答えなさい。\n\n     ア home                  イ remove            ウ gold                  エ notice\n\n\n\n問２   第一アクセント(第一強勢)の位置が他と異なるものを次の中から一つ選び、記号で答\n\n     えなさい。\n\n     ア award                 イ unique             ウ website             エ compare\n\n\n４    問１、問２が日本語の意味になるように [                           ] 内の語（句）を並べかえなさい。解\n     答欄には、（             ）内の１、２に入る語（句）を記号で答えなさい。\n\n\n問１   サリーはどうすれば自分が幸せになれるのかを探ろうとした。\n\n     Sally (     )( 1 )(        )(      )( 2 )(       )(      ).\n\n     [ア to / イ would make / ウ tried / エ find out / オ happy / カ her / キ what ]\n\n\n問２   ケンは人前で話すのが誰よりも得意だ。\n\n     Ken (      )( 1 )(        )(     )( 2 )(        )(     )(      ) of people.\n\n     [ ア front / イ speaking in / ウ anyone / エ better / オ is / カ than / キ else / ク at ]\n\n\n５    問１、問２の英文の空所に入る英語を一語ずつ答えなさい。ただし、与えられた文\n     字から始めること。\n\n問１   When a place is (c     ), it means there are a lot of people or too many people in it.\n\n問２   (G      ) is waste material or unwanted things that you throw away.\n\n\n                                             3"}, {"page": 7, "text": "６       次の会話を読み、会話文の Sally の空所に、Ken の意見をまとめた要約と、それに対\n\n        する反論を英語で書きなさい。語数は 50 語程度とする。\n\n\n\nSally     : Hi, Ken! Hurry up. The cleaning time will start soon!\n\nKen       : To be honest, I don’t enjoy cleaning time at school. Why do we have to do extra\n           work? We already have so much homework, club activities, and exams to study\n           for. It just feels like too much. I think it’s better to have professional cleaners. They\n           have experience and know how to do it well.\n\nSally     :(                               )\n\nKen       : Hmm… that’s a good point. I didn’t think about it that way.\n\nSally     : Also, if students clean the school, the school can save money and use it for\n            better things, like new books or equipment. I can think of lots of benefits!\n\nKen       : Okay… I guess it makes sense for us to clean.\n\n\n                                                  4"}, {"page": 8, "text": "７     次の英文を読んで、後の問１～６に答えなさい。\n\n       The box was by the front door. Norma Lewis picked it up and entered the apartment\n    as it got dark. She opened the box and found a push-button under a glass dome. A note\n    taped to the bottom read: Mr. Steward will visit you at 8:00 P.M.\n       At eight, the doorbell rang. Norma opened the door to a short man.\n       “Mrs. Lewis?” he asked. “I’m Mr. Steward.”\n       Norma was sure this was a sales visit.\n       “May I come in?” Mr. Steward asked.\n       “I’m busy,” Norma said. “I’ll return your box to you, though.” She started to turn.\n       “Don’t you want to know what it is? It could be very valuable,” he said.\n       “What are you trying to sell?” she asked.\n       “I’m not selling anything,” he replied.\n       Arthur Lewis came out of the living room. “Is there a problem?”\n       Mr. Steward introduced himself.\n       “Oh, the...” Arthur pointed toward the living room. “What is that thing, anyway?”\n       “It won’t take long to explain,” Mr. Steward replied. “May I come in?”\n       “Well, why not?” Arthur agreed.\n       They went into the living room. Mr. Steward sat and took a small envelope from his\n    pocket.\n       “Inside here is a key to the glass dome,” he said. “The button is connected to our office.”\n       “What’s it for?” Arthur asked.\n       “If you press the button,” Mr. Steward said, “somewhere in the world, someone you\n    don’t know will die. In return, you will receive $50,000.”\n       “Is this a joke?” Arthur asked.\n       “Not at all. The offer is real.”\n       “I think you should leave,” Arthur said, standing up.\n       Mr. Steward stood too. “Of course.”\n       “And take your box with you.”\n       “Don’t you want to think about it for a day or two?”\n       “①[         ]”\n       Arthur picked up the box and the envelope and gave them to Mr. Steward.\n       “I’ll leave my business card anyway,” Mr. Steward said. He placed it on the table by\n    the door.\n       When he was gone, Arthur tore* the card in half and threw the pieces on the table.\n       “Aren’t you even a little curious?” Norma asked.\n       “No,” he shook his head.\n\n      “Why won’t you talk about it?” Norma asked later as they were getting ready for bed.\n      “Doesn’t it make you wonder?”\n      “It ②[       ] me,” Arthur said.\n      “Maybe some rich person is doing a psychology experiment.”\n      “Maybe.”\n      “Don’t you want to know?”\n\n                                                  5"}, {"page": 9, "text": "  Arthur shook his head.\n  “Why not?”\n  “Because it’s wrong,” he said angrily.\n  The couple went to bed. Norma closed her eyes. ‘Fifty thousand dollars,’ she thought.\n\n   The next morning, as she left for work, Norma saw the pieces of the business card on\nthe table. ③She put them in her bag.\n   During her coffee break, she looked at the card again. It only had Mr. Steward’s name\nand phone number.\n   After lunch, she taped the card together and dialed the number.\n   “Good afternoon,” said Mr. Steward’s voice.\n   “This is Mrs. Lewis,” she said.\n   “Yes, Mrs. Lewis.” Mr. Steward sounded pleased.\n   “I’m curious.”\n   “That’s normal,” Mr. Steward said.\n   “When you said someone would die, what did you mean?”\n   “Exactly that,” he said. “It could be anyone. We only promise you that you won’t know\nthem. And of course, you won’t see them die.”\n   “For $50,000,” Norma said.\n   “That’s right.”\n   She laughed. “That can’t be true!”\n   “Still, that is the offer,” Mr. Steward said. “Would you like me to return the box?”\n   Norma said, “Of course not.” She hung up.\n\n   The box and the key were lying by the front door again when she returned home. She\nlooked at them. ‘I won’t bring them in,’ she thought. But later, she picked them up and left\nthem on the kitchen table.\n   Arthur came home and saw the box.\n   “I don’t understand,” he said.\n   “What do you mean?”\n   “Let it go,” he said angrily.\n   Norma was quiet, then suddenly asked, “What if it’s real?”\n   Arthur looked shocked. “Are you saying you’d press the button and kill a person?”\n   “Only if I didn’t know the person…” Norma answered.\n   Arthur was shocked. “Are you serious?”\n   “What if it’s some old man far away?” she continued.\n   “How about a child nearby?” Arthur asked.\n   “You’re making it emotional.”\n   “Norma,” he said, “it doesn’t matter who will ④[        ]. It’s still wrong.”\n   “The point is,” Norma said, “if you never see them, and don’t know them, does it really\nmatter?”\n   Arthur was horrified. “You would do it?”\n   “Fifty thousand dollars, Arthur.”\n   “Norma, no.”\n                                             6"}, {"page": 10, "text": "  “A trip to Europe.”\n  “Norma, no.”\n\n   ⑤The next morning, Norma made a big breakfast.\n   “What’s special today?” Arthur asked.\n   She filled his coffee cup. “I just wanted to show you I’m not... selfish. I think you\nmisunderstood me last night,” she said.\n   “How?”\n   “I wasn’t thinking only of myself. I meant things for both of us—travel, a nice home, a\nbetter car...”\n   “Norma, why are we talking about this again?”\n   “I’m just saying that I’d like a better life.”\n   “We will have one.” Arthur said.\n   “When?”\n   He looked upset.\n   “Are you really saying...”\n   “I’m saying maybe this is a psychological test. Maybe they just want to see what we\nwould do. You don’t think they’d really kill someone, do you?”\n   Arthur didn’t reply. After a while, he left for work.\n   Norma stayed at the table. She took the box from the kitchen and placed it on the table.\nAfter looking at it for a long time, she took the key, removed the dome, and pressed the\nbutton.\n   She felt a chill. Was something happening?\n   Then nothing. She laughed. ‘Silly,’ she thought. She threw the box in the trash can and\ngot ready for work. Then, the phone rang. She picked it up.\n   “Mrs. Lewis?”\n   “Yes?”\n   “This is Lenox Hill Hospital.”\n   They told her Arthur had died in a train accident.\n   She shook her head in disbelief. Then she remembered his life insurance*—$25,000,\ndoubled for accidents.\n   “No.”\n   She couldn’t breathe. The phone rang again. She answered.\n   “Mrs. Lewis?” Mr. Steward asked.\n   She screamed, “You said I wouldn’t know the one who died!”\n   Mr. Steward replied calmly: “My dear lady, do you really think you knew your husband?”\n\n       *tore: ‘tear’「やぶる」の過去形                    *insurance: 保険\n\n\n                                            7"}, {"page": 11, "text": "問１ 下線部①[      ]内に入る最も適切なものを次の中から一つ選び、記号で答えなさい。\n   ア Maybe, but let me talk to my wife first.\n     イ Sure, we’ll think about it.\n     ウ No, I’m not interested at all.\n     エ Okay, please leave it here.\n\n問２ 下線部②[            ]内に入る最も適切なものを次の中から一つ選び、記号で答えなさい。\n   ア upsets            イ surprises ウ interests エ bores\n\n問３ 下線部③の理由として最も適切なものを次の中から一つ選び、記号で答えなさい。\n     ア She was worried that she might change her mind later and wanted to throw\n       them away.\n     イ She was still curious about the offer that Mr. Steward had made and wanted\n       to know more about it.\n     ウ She was still angry that her husband had accepted Mr. Steward’s offer and\n       wanted to change his mind.\n     エ She was pleased with the kind offer and wanted to call Mr. Steward to thank\n       him.\n\n問4   下線部④[          ]内に入る最も適切な語を本文中から一語で抜き出し、答えなさい。\n\n問5   下線部⑤が表している状況として最も適切なものを次の中から一つ選び、記号で\n     答えなさい。\n     ア 特別な朝食を作ることで、夫が自分勝手な発言をしたことに罪悪感を持たせ\n       ようとしている様子。\n     イ 特別な朝食を作ることで、自分がボタンのことをもう気にしていないと伝え\n       ようとしている様子。\n     ウ 特別な朝食を作ることで、前日のけんかで夫を誤解したことについて謝罪し\n       ようとしている様子。\n     エ 特別な朝食を作ることで、自分は家族の幸せや未来のためを思って行動して\n       いるのだという気持ちを示そうとしている様子。\n\n問6   本文の内容と一致するものを次の中から一つ選び、記号で答えなさい。\n     ア Arthur invited Mr. Steward into the house after learning about what the box\n       was.\n     イ Mr. Steward explained that pressing the button would make any wish come\n       true.\n     ウ When Norma called Mr. Steward, she asked him to return the box to her\n       apartment.\n     エ Mr. Steward suggested that Norma may not have truly understood her\n       husband.\n\n\n                                        8"}, {"page": 12, "text": "８    次の英文を読んで、後の問１～６に答えなさい。\n     Weaver ants are special creatures that live in warm places like South Asia and\n\nNorthern Australia. They live in big groups and are famous for acting as a team. One\n\namazing behavior is cooperative transport. This means that ①many ants work [ 1 ] to\n\ncarry a large food item, [ 2 ] [ 3 ] a dead [ 4 ], back to their nest. This is difficult\n\nbecause all the ants must agree on which way to go. Humans can talk to make team\n\ndecisions, but ants cannot. So, how do ants decide where to go?\n\n     (ア) To find out, scientists studied how weaver ants make group ②[                ] when\n\nworking as a team. They wanted to know if the ants follow one leader, or if they all help\n\nmake a choice together.\n\n     (イ) To answer this question, they did an experiment called ③the tethered-object\n\ntest. In this test, the ants were given a piece of food — a grasshopper — but the\n\ngrasshopper was tied to the ground with a thin string. It was not easy to move. The\n\nresearchers looked at the direction the ants pulled and how often the group changed\n\ndirection. If the ants followed a leader, they would move in a regular pattern, like a wave.\n\n     (ウ) The results showed something very interesting. The ants did not follow a leader.\n\nInstead, each ant gave a little help in choosing the direction. This way of deciding is called\n\nthe ④“wisdom of the crowd.” When the group was small, their movement was random.\n\nHowever, with more ants introduced into the group, the group made better decisions. If\n\none ant made a mistake, the others corrected it. This teamwork helped them succeed.\n\n     (エ) These ants depend on a few individuals. Their decision-making process is\n\nfaster, but if the leader makes a mistake, the group can go the wrong way. Weaver ants,\n\nhowever, are more careful. They use their own knowledge and signals from others in the\n\ngroup to decide together. This makes their decision-making slower but more reliable,\n\nespecially when carrying large or live food.\n\n     This study can help people understand how to make better group decisions. It may\n\nbe useful for robots or teams that need to work without a leader. By learning from ants,\n\nwe can improve teamwork in many areas.\n\n\n\n                                               9"}, {"page": 13, "text": "問１ 下線部①について、次の日本語とほぼ同じ内容になるように、空所に入る英語を\n   一語ずつ答えなさい。\n  「多くのアリが、死んだ虫などの大きな食べ物を巣に協力して持ち帰る」\n\n\n問２ 下線部②[          ]に入る最も適切な語を本文中から一語で抜き出し、答えなさい。\n\n\n問３ 下線部③において行われていないものを次の中から一つ選び、記号で答えなさい。\n  ア preparing food                     イ adjusting the group size of ants\n  ウ observing movements                エ introducing enemies\n\n\n問４ 下線部④の説明として最も適切なものを次の中から一つ選び、記号で答えなさい。\n  ア A group can make better decisions than an individual by working together.\n  イ A group can make better decisions than an individual by following a good leader.\n  ウ An individual can make decisions faster than a group when working alone.\n  エ An individual can make decisions faster than a group when correcting mistakes.\n\n\n問５ 次の英文を入れるのに最も適切な箇所を本文中の(ア) ～ (エ)の中から一つ選び、\n  記号で答えなさい。\n  This is different from other ants, like longhorn crazy ants, which follow a leader.\n\n\n問６ 本文の内容と一致するものを次の中から一つ選び、記号で答えなさい。\n  ア Weaver ants move large items while talking to each other as human beings do.\n  イ The scientists found that weaver ants followed one leader, which made\n      decisions for the group.\n  ウ Group decisions are not reliable when weaver ants carry large or live food items.\n  エ This study may be able to help people learn how to make good group decisions\n      without needing a leader.\n\n\n                                         10"}]};
window.DRILLS=[{"id": "pr01", "skill": "pronunciation", "level": 1, "type": "choice", "prompt": "下線部 ea の発音が他と異なるものを選びなさい。", "options": ["seat", "dead", "team", "clean"], "answer": 1, "explanation": "dead の ea は /e/、他は /iː/。"}, {"id": "pr02", "skill": "pronunciation", "level": 1, "type": "choice", "prompt": "下線部 ou の発音が他と異なるものを選びなさい。", "options": ["young", "touch", "country", "group"], "answer": 3, "explanation": "group は /uː/、他は /ʌ/。"}, {"id": "pr03", "skill": "pronunciation", "level": 1, "type": "choice", "prompt": "下線部 o の発音が他と異なるものを選びなさい。", "options": ["home", "gold", "notice", "come"], "answer": 3, "explanation": "come は /ʌ/、他は /oʊ/。"}, {"id": "pr04", "skill": "pronunciation", "level": 2, "type": "choice", "prompt": "太字 g の発音が他と異なるものを選びなさい。", "options": ["gesture", "giant", "gym", "goat"], "answer": 3, "explanation": "goat は /g/、他は /dʒ/。"}, {"id": "pr05", "skill": "pronunciation", "level": 2, "type": "choice", "prompt": "下線部 th の発音が他と異なるものを選びなさい。", "options": ["this", "those", "think", "there"], "answer": 2, "explanation": "think は /θ/、他は /ð/。"}, {"id": "pr06", "skill": "pronunciation", "level": 2, "type": "choice", "prompt": "下線部 ea の発音が他と異なるものを選びなさい。", "options": ["breathe", "meat", "spread", "seat"], "answer": 2, "explanation": "spread は /e/、他は /iː/。"}, {"id": "pr07", "skill": "pronunciation", "level": 2, "type": "choice", "prompt": "下線部 i の発音が他と異なるものを選びなさい。", "options": ["bird", "first", "girl", "mirror"], "answer": 3, "explanation": "mirror の i は /ɪ/、他は /ɜːr/。"}, {"id": "pr08", "skill": "pronunciation", "level": 3, "type": "choice", "prompt": "語末 -ed の発音が他と異なるものを選びなさい。", "options": ["wanted", "needed", "visited", "played"], "answer": 3, "explanation": "played は /d/、他は /ɪd/。"}, {"id": "st01", "skill": "stress", "level": 1, "type": "choice", "prompt": "第一アクセントの位置が他と異なるものを選びなさい。", "options": ["damage", "coffee", "special", "receive"], "answer": 3, "explanation": "receive は第2音節、他は第1音節。"}, {"id": "st02", "skill": "stress", "level": 1, "type": "choice", "prompt": "第一アクセントの位置が他と異なるものを選びなさい。", "options": ["problem", "climate", "weather", "reduce"], "answer": 3, "explanation": "reduce は第2音節、他は第1音節。"}, {"id": "st03", "skill": "stress", "level": 2, "type": "choice", "prompt": "第一アクセントの位置が他と異なるものを選びなさい。", "options": ["continue", "decide", "return", "passenger"], "answer": 3, "explanation": "passenger は第1音節、他は第2音節。"}, {"id": "st04", "skill": "stress", "level": 2, "type": "choice", "prompt": "第一アクセントの位置が他と異なるものを選びなさい。", "options": ["musician", "delicious", "official", "character"], "answer": 3, "explanation": "character は第1音節、他は後ろ寄りに第一アクセント。"}, {"id": "st05", "skill": "stress", "level": 2, "type": "choice", "prompt": "第一アクセントの位置が他と異なるものを選びなさい。", "options": ["volunteer", "Japanese", "engineer", "government"], "answer": 3, "explanation": "government は第1音節、他は語末寄り。"}, {"id": "st06", "skill": "stress", "level": 3, "type": "choice", "prompt": "第一アクセントの位置が他と異なるものを選びなさい。", "options": ["expensive", "important", "computer", "festival"], "answer": 3, "explanation": "festival は第1音節、他は第2音節。"}, {"id": "ro01", "skill": "reorder", "level": 1, "type": "reorder", "prompt": "私は彼女に何と言えばよいかわからない。", "lead": "I", "tokens": ["have", "no", "idea", "what", "I", "should", "say", "to her"], "answer": ["have", "no", "idea", "what", "I", "should", "say", "to her"], "explanation": "have no idea + 疑問詞 + S + should + 動詞。"}, {"id": "ro02", "skill": "reorder", "level": 1, "type": "reorder", "prompt": "夜更かしをする人は風邪をひきやすい。", "lead": "People", "tokens": ["who", "stay", "up", "late", "may", "catch", "a cold", "easily"], "answer": ["who", "stay", "up", "late", "may", "catch", "a cold", "easily"], "explanation": "people who ... の関係代名詞節を先に作る。"}, {"id": "ro03", "skill": "reorder", "level": 1, "type": "reorder", "prompt": "彼は私にそのクラブに入っているかどうか尋ねた。", "lead": "He", "tokens": ["asked", "me", "if", "I", "belonged", "to", "the club"], "answer": ["asked", "me", "if", "I", "belonged", "to", "the club"], "explanation": "ask 人 if S V。"}, {"id": "ro04", "skill": "reorder", "level": 2, "type": "reorder", "prompt": "何が彼を日本へ連れてきたのか知っていますか。", "lead": "Do you", "tokens": ["know", "what", "brought", "him", "to", "Japan"], "answer": ["know", "what", "brought", "him", "to", "Japan"], "explanation": "what 自体が brought の主語なので what did bring とはしない。"}, {"id": "ro05", "skill": "reorder", "level": 2, "type": "reorder", "prompt": "彼は休暇中に犬の世話をしてくれる人を探している。", "lead": "He is", "tokens": ["looking", "for", "someone", "who", "can", "take care", "of his dog"], "answer": ["looking", "for", "someone", "who", "can", "take care", "of his dog"], "explanation": "look for someone who can ... / take care of。"}, {"id": "ro06", "skill": "reorder", "level": 2, "type": "reorder", "prompt": "私たちは学園祭の準備に多くの努力を注いだ。", "lead": "We", "tokens": ["put", "a lot", "of", "effort", "into", "preparing", "for", "the festival"], "answer": ["put", "a lot", "of", "effort", "into", "preparing", "for", "the festival"], "explanation": "put effort into -ing。"}, {"id": "ro07", "skill": "reorder", "level": 2, "type": "reorder", "prompt": "その情報がどこから来たのか調べた方がよい。", "lead": "We should", "tokens": ["find", "out", "where", "the information", "comes", "from"], "answer": ["find", "out", "where", "the information", "comes", "from"], "explanation": "find out + where S comes from。"}, {"id": "ro08", "skill": "reorder", "level": 2, "type": "reorder", "prompt": "彼はレポートを終えたが、病気になったため提出できなかった。", "lead": "He finished his report,", "tokens": ["but", "he couldn't", "hand", "it", "in", "because", "he got", "sick"], "answer": ["but", "he couldn't", "hand", "it", "in", "because", "he got", "sick"], "explanation": "hand it in / because S V。"}, {"id": "ro09", "skill": "reorder", "level": 3, "type": "reorder", "prompt": "サリーは何が自分を幸せにするのかを探ろうとした。", "lead": "Sally", "tokens": ["tried", "to", "find out", "what", "would make", "her", "happy"], "answer": ["tried", "to", "find out", "what", "would make", "her", "happy"], "explanation": "try to find out + what would make O C。"}, {"id": "ro10", "skill": "reorder", "level": 3, "type": "reorder", "prompt": "ケンは人前で話すのが誰よりも得意だ。", "lead": "Ken", "tokens": ["is", "better", "at", "speaking", "in front", "of people", "than", "anyone else"], "answer": ["is", "better", "at", "speaking", "in front", "of people", "than", "anyone else"], "explanation": "be better at -ing than anyone else。"}, {"id": "co01", "skill": "connector", "level": 1, "type": "choice", "prompt": "Some bacteria are harmful, ___ others can be useful if used wisely.", "options": ["while", "because", "so", "if"], "answer": 0, "explanation": "対比なので while。"}, {"id": "co02", "skill": "connector", "level": 1, "type": "choice", "prompt": "The road was closed, ___ we had to take another route.", "options": ["but", "so", "while", "if"], "answer": 1, "explanation": "原因→結果なので so。"}, {"id": "co03", "skill": "connector", "level": 2, "type": "choice", "prompt": "The experiment failed once. ___, the team changed the method and tried again.", "options": ["However", "For example", "Because", "Unless"], "answer": 0, "explanation": "逆接・展開の転換。"}, {"id": "pa01", "skill": "paraphrase", "level": 1, "type": "choice", "prompt": "“be resistant to the drug” に最も近い意味は？", "options": ["be killed easily by it", "not be killed by it", "be made from it", "be afraid of it"], "answer": 1, "explanation": "resistant = 効きにくい、抵抗性がある。"}, {"id": "pa02", "skill": "paraphrase", "level": 2, "type": "choice", "prompt": "“no good to you” に最も近い意味は？", "options": ["not useful to you", "too expensive for you", "not known to you", "not allowed for you"], "answer": 0, "explanation": "no good = 役に立たない。"}, {"id": "pa03", "skill": "paraphrase", "level": 2, "type": "choice", "prompt": "“the plan did more harm than good” に最も近い意味は？", "options": ["It helped everyone.", "It caused more problems than benefits.", "It was cheap and simple.", "It was never tried."], "answer": 1, "explanation": "harm と good の比較を言い換える。"}, {"id": "re01", "skill": "reference", "level": 1, "type": "choice", "prompt": "Mika found a note on the desk. She read it twice before putting it in her bag. “it” は何を指す？", "options": ["the desk", "the note", "her bag", "Mika"], "answer": 1, "explanation": "直前の単数名詞 the note。"}, {"id": "re02", "skill": "reference", "level": 2, "type": "choice", "prompt": "The researchers discovered a new method. This could help doctors work faster. “This” の内容は？", "options": ["the researchers", "the new method/discovery", "doctors", "working slowly"], "answer": 1, "explanation": "前文全体の発見内容を受ける。"}, {"id": "cx01", "skill": "context", "level": 1, "type": "choice", "prompt": "Aki checked the mailbox but found nothing. She was ___ because she had expected an important letter.", "options": ["disappointed", "relieved", "proud", "amused"], "answer": 0, "explanation": "期待していたのに何もない→disappointed。"}, {"id": "cx02", "skill": "context", "level": 2, "type": "choice", "prompt": "The room was completely silent. Then a loud crash came from upstairs. Everyone suddenly became ___.", "options": ["sleepy", "nervous", "hungry", "bored"], "answer": 1, "explanation": "突然の大きな音という文脈から nervous。"}, {"id": "em01", "skill": "emotion", "level": 1, "type": "choice", "prompt": "Tom thought his lost dog had returned when he heard scratching at the door. He ran to open it, but found only a branch moving in the wind. Tom most likely felt...", "options": ["relieved then excited", "hopeful then disappointed", "angry then proud", "bored then sleepy"], "answer": 1, "explanation": "期待→現実を知って落胆、の心情変化。"}, {"id": "em02", "skill": "emotion", "level": 2, "type": "choice", "prompt": "Maya was afraid to give her speech. Afterward, several classmates told her it was clear and interesting. She smiled all the way home. Her feeling changed from...", "options": ["fear to confidence", "anger to fear", "joy to sadness", "surprise to boredom"], "answer": 0, "explanation": "発表前の不安から、成功後の自信へ。"}, {"id": "rs01", "skill": "reason", "level": 1, "type": "choice", "prompt": "Ben refused to open the old box because the owner had warned him that it was dangerous. Why did Ben refuse?", "options": ["He had lost the key.", "He was following a warning.", "He wanted to sell it.", "He did not see the box."], "answer": 1, "explanation": "because 以下が直接の理由。"}, {"id": "rs02", "skill": "reason", "level": 2, "type": "choice", "prompt": "The team changed the experiment after the first results were inconsistent. Why did they change it?", "options": ["They wanted more reliable results.", "They had finished the study.", "They disliked the equipment color.", "They wanted fewer participants."], "answer": 0, "explanation": "inconsistent results → reliability を高めるため。"}, {"id": "dt01", "skill": "detail", "level": 1, "type": "choice", "prompt": "Rina left home at 7:20, walked 10 minutes to the station, and took a 25-minute train. What time did she reach the destination station?", "options": ["7:45", "7:55", "8:05", "8:15"], "answer": 1, "explanation": "7:20 + 10分 + 25分 = 7:55。"}, {"id": "dt02", "skill": "detail", "level": 2, "type": "choice", "prompt": "The study used 24 students. Half read in silence and the other half listened to music. How many students were in each group?", "options": ["6", "10", "12", "24"], "answer": 2, "explanation": "24の半分は12。"}, {"id": "ex01", "skill": "example", "level": 1, "type": "choice", "prompt": "“Small rewards can encourage animals to repeat an action.” の具体例として最も適切なのは？", "options": ["A bee receives sugar water after touching a target.", "A bird sleeps in a tree.", "A dog sees a red ball.", "A fish swims at night."], "answer": 0, "explanation": "reward と repeat an action の両方が入る例。"}, {"id": "ex02", "skill": "example", "level": 2, "type": "choice", "prompt": "“Technology can reduce unnecessary work.” の具体例として最も適切なのは？", "options": ["A machine automatically counts hundreds of items.", "A student writes the same list twice.", "A worker turns off the computer.", "A teacher buys a new desk."], "answer": 0, "explanation": "自動化で不要な作業を減らす例。"}, {"id": "in01", "skill": "insertion", "level": 1, "type": "choice", "prompt": "文挿入：『For this reason, the team decided to repeat the test.』最も自然な位置は？\nA. The first result was very different from the others. [1] They checked the equipment. [2] The second test produced a similar result. [3]", "options": ["[1]", "[2]", "[3]", "どこにも入らない"], "answer": 0, "explanation": "this reason は『最初の結果が他と違った』ことを受ける。"}, {"id": "in02", "skill": "insertion", "level": 2, "type": "choice", "prompt": "文挿入：『However, this solution also creates a new problem.』最も自然な位置は？\nA new road can reduce traffic in the center. [1] Building it may destroy part of a park. [2] City leaders must compare both effects. [3]", "options": ["[1]", "[2]", "[3]", "どこにも入らない"], "answer": 0, "explanation": "メリットの後に However でデメリットへ転換。"}, {"id": "in03", "skill": "insertion", "level": 2, "type": "choice", "prompt": "文挿入：『The researchers then tested this idea with a second group.』最も自然な位置は？\nThey first developed a possible explanation. [1] The second group showed the same pattern. [2] This made the explanation more convincing. [3]", "options": ["[1]", "[2]", "[3]", "どこにも入らない"], "answer": 0, "explanation": "idea を受けて second group の結果につなぐ。"}, {"id": "cm01", "skill": "content_match", "level": 1, "type": "choice", "prompt": "本文：Leo planned to visit London, but changed his plan and went to Rome. 一致するものは？", "options": ["Leo visited London.", "Leo originally planned London.", "Leo never changed his plan.", "Leo went to Paris."], "answer": 1, "explanation": "originally planned と実際に行った場所を区別する。"}, {"id": "cm02", "skill": "content_match", "level": 2, "type": "choice", "prompt": "本文：The medicine helped most patients, but it did not work for everyone. 一致するものは？", "options": ["It helped every patient.", "It helped no one.", "It helped many, not all.", "The study did not use medicine."], "answer": 2, "explanation": "most ≠ all。"}, {"id": "cm03", "skill": "content_match", "level": 2, "type": "choice", "prompt": "本文：At first, the students wanted to cancel the event. After the weather improved, they decided to hold it outdoors. 一致するものは？", "options": ["The event was canceled.", "The final decision was to hold it outdoors.", "The weather became worse.", "They never changed their decision."], "answer": 1, "explanation": "at first と final decision を区別する。"}, {"id": "et01", "skill": "extract", "level": 1, "type": "text", "prompt": "本文：The children were exhausted after walking for five hours. 『疲れ果てた』に当たる1語を抜き出しなさい。", "answerText": "exhausted", "explanation": "本文中の形容詞 exhausted。"}, {"id": "et02", "skill": "extract", "level": 1, "type": "text", "prompt": "本文：The new rule was introduced to reduce unnecessary waste. 『減らす』に当たる1語を抜き出しなさい。", "answerText": "reduce", "explanation": "本文中の動詞 reduce。"}, {"id": "et03", "skill": "extract", "level": 2, "type": "text", "prompt": "本文：The result was unexpected, so the scientists repeated the experiment. 『予想外の』に当たる1語を抜き出しなさい。", "answerText": "unexpected", "explanation": "本文中の unexpected。"}, {"id": "sc01", "skill": "sentence_completion", "level": 1, "type": "text_multi", "prompt": "『その薬のおかげで人々は再び元気になることができる。』\nThe medicine can (1) people (2) well again.", "answers": ["help", "get"], "explanation": "help + 人 + 動詞原形 / get well。"}, {"id": "sc02", "skill": "sentence_completion", "level": 2, "type": "text_multi", "prompt": "『それがなければ危険になる可能性がある。』\n(1) it, the situation may become (2).", "answers": ["Without", "dangerous"], "explanation": "Without it / become dangerous。"}, {"id": "sc03", "skill": "sentence_completion", "level": 2, "type": "text_multi", "prompt": "『一緒に働くことでより良い決定ができる。』\nWorking (1) can help us make better (2).", "answers": ["together", "decisions"], "explanation": "work together / make decisions。"}, {"id": "wr01", "skill": "writing_completion", "level": 1, "type": "selfcheck", "prompt": "短文完成：A boy blamed his friend for losing a key, but later found the key in his own bag. Complete the lesson in 12 words or fewer.", "check": ["自分の思い込みに気づく内容", "12語以内", "主語＋動詞がある"], "model": "We should check the facts before blaming other people.", "explanation": "2019〜2021型：物語の教訓を短くまとめる。"}, {"id": "su01", "skill": "summary", "level": 1, "type": "selfcheck", "prompt": "40〜50語で要約：A woman thought glasses would help her read, but after trying many pairs she still could not read because she had never learned how.", "check": ["人物", "試したこと", "うまくいかなかった理由", "40〜50語を大きく外れない"], "model": "A woman thought she could read if she wore glasses, so she tried many pairs. However, none of them helped because the real problem was not her eyesight. She had never learned how to read.", "explanation": "2022〜2023型：原因と結論を残す。"}, {"id": "su02", "skill": "summary", "level": 2, "type": "selfcheck", "prompt": "45〜55語で要約：Ken got a lot of money and first felt happy, but he became worried about losing it. He finally returned it and felt peaceful again.", "check": ["最初の状況", "お金を得た", "心情の悪化", "返して平穏を取り戻す"], "model": "Ken received a large amount of money and was happy at first. Soon, however, he began to worry about losing it and could not relax. In the end, he returned the money and became peaceful again.", "explanation": "出来事→心情変化→結末。"}, {"id": "rb01", "skill": "rebuttal", "level": 1, "type": "selfcheck", "prompt": "約50語：『学校の掃除はプロに任せるべき。生徒は忙しい』という意見を要約し、反論しなさい。", "check": ["相手の主張を要約", "However/But", "自分の反論", "理由または具体例"], "model": "You are saying that students are too busy, so professional cleaners should clean the school. However, I think students should do some cleaning themselves. It teaches them to take care of shared places and work with others, and it can also reduce school costs.", "explanation": "2024〜2026型：要約→転換→反論→理由。"}, {"id": "rb02", "skill": "rebuttal", "level": 2, "type": "selfcheck", "prompt": "約50語：『スマートフォンは授業の集中を妨げるので学校で禁止すべき』という意見を要約し、反論しなさい。", "check": ["禁止理由を正確に要約", "転換", "条件付きの反論", "具体的ルール"], "model": "You are saying that smartphones should be banned because they can distract students. However, they can be useful if schools set clear rules. Students can use them only for research or emergencies, while games and social media remain prohibited during lessons.", "explanation": "全面否定より『ルールを設ければ有用』が書きやすい。"}, {"id": "rb03", "skill": "rebuttal", "level": 2, "type": "selfcheck", "prompt": "約50語：『学校行事は費用がかかるので減らすべき』という意見を要約し、反論しなさい。", "check": ["費用の問題を要約", "However/But", "反論", "学習・協力などの具体的利点"], "model": "You are saying that schools should reduce events because they cost too much. However, events can give students experiences that ordinary lessons cannot. They can learn to cooperate, plan, and communicate with others. Schools can control costs by choosing simpler activities instead of canceling them.", "explanation": "相手の理由を受けた上で、代替策を示すと論理が安定。"}];
window.FALLBACK={"detail": ["reason", "reference", "content_match"], "context": ["emotion", "paraphrase"], "reason": ["detail", "emotion"], "example": ["detail"], "reference": ["detail"], "paraphrase": ["context"], "connector": ["insertion"], "insertion": ["connector"], "vocab_definition": ["extract"]};
